description:
## Why
Plan: `docs/FORENSIC_CASE_STUDIES_PLAN.md` §15 A7a, §3.1, §3.3, §3.4, §3.5, §16.

The runner is the only engine-touching code in the program (§3.1). It turns the authored data into executions of the real `fa` binary and turns robot output into verdicts. `CARGO_BIN_EXE_fa` exists only for integration tests of the `fa-cli` package, so the runner is the integration test target `crates/fa-cli/tests/cases.rs` (§3.1). It is a walking skeleton: an expectation whose required witness is not implemented yet prints UNRUN, never PASS. Honesty is enforced per row by the runner, not by withholding the runner (§15, rules for every item). This item serves every claim type, because every expectation is evaluated here.

## What to build
**Files** (introduced here):
- `crates/fa-cli/Cargo.toml`: `[dev-dependencies] fa-oracles = { path = "../fa-oracles", version = "=0.0.1" }`. Dev edges into the verification layer are lawful (topology header; precedent `crates/fa-lantern/Cargo.toml:31`); fa-cli's topology row has no allowlist.
- `crates/fa-cli/tests/cases.rs`: one `#[test]` per case, named after its directory (§3.2b: `fn _selftest()` first; later `fn worldcom()`, `fn xbrl()`, …; run one with `cargo test -p fa-cli --test cases -- --exact worldcom`), one per cross-cutting check (§12), and the permanent run-time planted negatives `fn <dir>_neg_<slug>()`. Helpers in `crates/fa-cli/tests/cases/{run,book,replay,observe,evaluate}.rs`, included with `#[path = "cases/<name>.rs"] mod <name>;` (precedent `crates/fa-cli/tests/proofs.rs:6-9`).
- In fa-oracles (engine-independent, so the renderer and A13 read the same formats):
  - `src/json.rs`: a minimal JSON reader (objects, arrays, strings with `\u` escapes, i128 integers, `true`/`false`/`null`; a float or a duplicate key is an error), the "own minimal JSON reader" of §3.1;
  - `src/cases/observe.rs`: `pub struct Observation { event, book, coordinate, data, certificate, error: Option<ObservedError> }` and `pub fn Observation::from_robot_line(line: &str) -> Result<Observation, ObserveError>` (§3.1). `ObservedError` keeps the `error` event's `class`, `exit`, `refusal_id` and the `refusal` object (`id`, `code`, `exit`, `why`, `fix.argv`, `alternatives`; the `refusal_object` shape of `registries/verbs.toml`);
  - `src/cases/log.rs`: `pub struct StepRecord` (§3.5 fields plus `record = "step"`, `binds`, `reference`) and `pub struct VerdictRecord` (`record = "verdict"`, `case`, `expectation`, `type`, `book_variant`, `target`, `cell`, `verdict`, `projection`, `witnesses`, `reason`), one per printed row (step 7), each with `to_line()` and `parse_line()`;
  - `src/cases/verdict.rs`: `pub enum RowVerdict { Pass, Fail, Unrun }` and the line formatter;
  - `src/cases/stub.rs`: the citation-stub renderer (`fa:case-source-stub:v1`, §2.7) and the placeholder renderer (`fa:case-placeholder:v1`);
  - `src/cases/intent_json.rs`: renders an `[[intent]]` and its legs, in `[[leg]] ordinal` order, to the JSON the CLI accepts (`crates/fa-cli/src/cli.rs:7339-7363`), and a staged migration intent's legacy attribution (`source_entered_by`, `source_approved_by`, A1b) into the chunk JSON that `fa migrate stage` seals (`cli.rs:21397-21404` at 8196f5e40); derived, never committed (§3.1);
  - `src/cases/selfrow.rs`: generates the `<case>-SELF-<variant>` rows;
  - `src/cases/remedy.rs`: the P structured-remedy check, deriving the admissible classes and ops per (class, state) from `registries/admissibility.toml`'s `[[cell]]` rows, read raw with `fa_registry::toml::parse` (an independent derivation, §5.2);
  - `src/cases/harness_verbs.rs`: `pub const HARNESS_VERBS: &[(&str, Option<&str>)]`, every (verb, subcommand) the runner invokes;
  - `src/cases/fixtures.rs`: `pub fn <name>() -> CaseFiles` for every runner fixture (`include_str!` of `crates/fa-oracles/tests/fixtures/<name>/…`), so the runner never reads another crate's files and every fixture edit stays inside fa-oracles' hashed tree (`scripts/crate_verdicts.py:152-179`).
- `crates/fa-oracles/cases/_selftest/` (`case.toml` with `kind = "harness_selftest"`, `roles.toml`, `books/native.toml`, `expectations.toml`) and its lines in `src/cases/embedded.rs`: no sources, never rendered, exempt from K-BASIS by kind. It is seeded with every stage this item implements; A8a completes it.

**Pipeline (§3.3), in this item.**
1. **Load and validate**, purely, with no binary: `fa_oracles::cases::load` and `run_load_checks` (A3a, plus the checks of other items as they land, and the case-scoped checks registered in A3a's `CASE_CHECKS` for this case, such as X5's). A `FAIL` load check stops the case before any book is created; the case's verdict lines then all read FAIL with the load failure as reason.
2. **Check the verb table.** Every `HARNESS_VERBS` entry must be a verb (and subcommand) of `fa_cli::verbs::registry()`, so a renamed verb fails before any book is created (§3.3).
3. **Create the book** in a fresh retained directory under `std::env::temp_dir()` (the `tmp()` pattern of `crates/fa-cli/tests/cli.rs:98-112`; never deleted, so a reproduce argv stays runnable):
   - `fa book create --name <case id> --calendar custom --periods <periods.json> --opening-date <d> [--entity <code>[:<name>]]… --json` (the entities from A1b's optional `[book] entities`), with `periods.json` rendered from `[[period]]` as `{"years":[{"fiscal_year":N,"periods":[{"start":…,"end":…}]}]}` (the usage at `registries/verbs.toml`, `book create … --calendar custom`); the `book_created` event's `book_id` is logged;
   - `fa period create --fiscal-year N` for each year, then `fa period list --json`; each `[[period]] code` label maps to the created period whose (`start`, `end`) equal the row's (the `period` shape carries `code`, `start`, `end`), and a label with no unique match fails the case before replay. Every later argv uses the reported code; the map is logged once per book;
   - no period is opened here. Periods are born `Future`, which admits nothing (`registries/admissibility.toml:55`), and only the history's `period_open` steps open them (step 4; the custom-calendar flow is `fa period create` then `fa period open`, `crates/fa-cli/tests/cli.rs:15466-15530`). So a book may keep a `Future` period on purpose (A16's probe reads one), and a missing `period_open` step is observed as the refusal it causes, never repaired by the runner (W3's `worldcom_neg_missing_period_open`). A migration book's pre-opening periods are born by admissibility's `[[pre_opening]]` rule and never opened (A7m);
   - principals via `--as <label>` and `--grant <op>` per invocation. There is no principal registry, and issued tokens cannot approve on the CLI (`FA-REF-011`);
   - chart: `fa chart import <chart.json>` rendered from `[[account]]`, then `fa chart role` for each non-empty role;
   - parties: `fa party add` per `[[party]]` row (its `id` as the party code), then `fa party relate <from> <to> --kind … --basis … --valid-from … [--valid-to …] [--pct …]` per `relations` token (A1b's grammar; `crates/fa-cli/src/rea.rs:23`);
   - the provenance engagement: `fa engagement open <id> --kind investigation --period <code>… --overall "0.00 <BASE>" --performance "0.00 <BASE>" --clearly-trivial "0.00 <BASE>"` (`registries/verbs.toml:736`), into which each `engagement_file` step files its document (`fa engagement file <id> --document <oid>`) or its findings (step 4) at its place in the history;
   - citation stubs: for each `[[document]]` whose `stub_of` is a source or fact id, the stub's canonical bytes (`fa:case-source-stub:v1`, then one `key=value` line each for `id`, `title`, `accession`, `original_url`, `sha256`, `assertion_status` and `retrieval_route`; a fact stub adds `anchor` and `quote_sha256`; §2.7) are written to a file and added with `fa document add <file> --kind Other` (default grade `AgentUpload`, `crates/fa-cli/src/document.rs:363-371`, never `HumanUpload`), each followed by `fa document attest review <oid> --as case-second-reader` when `reviewed_by` is set. The one `Disclosure`-kind stub per restatement case is A7b's.
4. **Replay the documented history** in `[[step]]` order:
   - an `intent` step: `fa post --intent <rendered.json> --as <principal> [--grant <op>]… [--evidence <oid>]… --json`;
   - an intent whose evidence is `manual_assertion:<code>`: `fa document attest manual --intent <rendered.json> --reason <code> --statement "<generated>" --plan-out <p>`, then `fa commit --plan <p>` (`crates/fa-cli/src/document.rs:1711-1765`; usage `registries/verbs.toml:483`). A second `fa post --intent` would reseal the description with fresh entropy (`crates/fa-cli/src/cli.rs:8137`), and the assertion would no longer cover its proposal. The statement is generated from the fact id ("reconstruction placeholder: the record describes this document; its bytes are not public"), never free text;
   - an `allocate` step: `fa allocate --intent <alloc.json> --as <principal> [--grant <op>]… [--evidence <oid>]… --json`, the allocation JSON rendered from the intent's `allocation_law` (as `method`, which the CLI otherwise defaults to largest remainder, `crates/fa-cli/src/cli.rs:9776-9778` at 8196f5e40; a rendering without it is refused before the binary runs, so a default is never chosen silently: W9's `EqualSplitResidualFirst`), `allocation_total` (`total`), `allocation_source` and `allocation_source_side` (`source`, `source_side`: `cli.rs:10142`, `:10306`), `effective_date` and `allocation_targets`; target legs are never authored, because the law computes them (W-T-04);
   - a `batch` step: `fa batch --intent <batch.json> --as <principal> --json` over its intent range, or, on a `ledger` stem, over the rows of one `[[ledger_map]]` account rendered as two-leg intents (M0's mapping, ids `<account>:<page>:<line>`, at most 8,190 entries, `registries/limits.toml:99-101`, with M0's derived key), one JSONL record per batch;
   - period transitions: `fa period open <code>` (a `period_open` step); `fa period soft-close`; `fa period close --reason … --yes`; `fa period lock --plan … --approval <vid>`, where the vid is printed by `fa approve --proposal <digest> --as <another principal>`. Who acts is the step's `principal` and who approves its `approvers` (A1b's step fields and their plan-role defaults); the runner never picks an actor itself. All approval production goes through one helper, `approve_as(label, digest)` (see the fa-z46b note);
   - an `engagement_file` step whose `ref` is `finding:<expectation id>` (A1b): `fa engagement file <engagement> --finding <vid>` for every finding the runner matched to that expectation's targets in this run (`registries/verbs.toml:736`), after its `finding_record` step;
   - an `event_record` step: `fa event record --code <id> --kind <kind> --occurred-on <day> --evidence <oid>… [--participant <party>]… [--resource <label>:<amount>]` from the `[[event]]` row (A1b; `registries/verbs.toml:499-507` at 8196f5e40);
   - `rule_check`, `rule_propose`, `rule_backtest` steps: `fa rule check <file>`, `fa rule propose <file>` and `fa rule backtest <file> --compare <digest> --from <day> --to <day>` (`registries/verbs.toml:656-664` at 8196f5e40), where the step's `compare=<seq>` param binds `<digest>` to the IR digest that `rule_propose` step's `rule_proposed` event reported (a placeholder in `binds`);
   - `migrate_import` steps (A1b's `[[migration_import]]` table): `<import id>` runs `fa migrate import --action prepare --intent <rendered import.json>`, and `<import id>#<chunk>` runs `--action stage --package D:<the package oid that import's prepare step reported> --chunk <chunk> --approval <v1> --approval <v2>` (`crates/fa-cli/src/migration_import.rs:94-106`); a `rows:<fact>` source document is rendered from the committed `rows_file` as the generic csv the import's `column_map` reads, and while that rows file is not committed (OD 2b) every step that needs it prints UNRUN `reason=rows file not committed (OD 2b)`;
   - refusals are observations, each captured with its exit code, refusal id and refusal object;
   - an approval encoded as an entry approval needs A0. The runner tests for it in the registry (`fa_cli::verbs::registry()`: `post` lists `--approval`); until it does, the row is UNRUN with `reason=needs:A0 (fa post --approval)`, never faked through the library. When A0 lands, plain entry-approval rows run without a runner change. An intent that carries both an entry approval and `manual_assertion:<code>` evidence needs A0's review form: one seal (`fa post --intent <rendered.json> --review-out <r>`), `fa approve --proposal <proposal_digest> --as <approver>`, then `fa document attest manual --review <r> --digest <review_digest> --reason <code> --statement "<generated>" --approval <vid> --plan-out <p>` and `fa commit --plan <p>`, so the approval and the assertion cover the same proposal (A0). That route cannot be exercised before A0 exists, so it is added to the runner with the first claimed cell that needs it (W5b, E3b); it is detected the same way (the `document` row lists `--review`), and until then such a row is UNRUN `reason=needs:A0 (attest manual --review)`.
5. **Run the audit program.** `fa procedure run <id> --known-at <seq>`, `fa jet …` and `fa finding record <id> --known-at <seq> [--param <name>=<integer>]…` for each claimed procedure. `<seq>` is the recorded sequence the runner observed at the step position A1c's `KnownAt::frontier(book, step)` resolves the step's `known_at` to: `head` is the head at the step (W-D-07/W-D-08 only); `pre_case` is the recorded head just before the variant's first `case_open` step, and on a variant with no `[[case]]` it is the head at the `finding_record` step (W4's migration book after `fa migrate finalize`; M5's and M7's ledger book after its last batch). A `finding_record` step's `params` (A1b's field, where a step carries one; L4's `window_days=4`) are passed as one `--param` each (`registries/verbs.toml`, `finding record` usage `[--param <name=integer>]...`); a step without them runs on the registered defaults. D components are read with `fa finding list --certificate C:<run>`.
6. **Read** `fa tb --period <code> --json`.
7. **Evaluate** expectations per component status (§5), one row per evaluated (expectation, book variant, target, cell): an expectation with several targets or branch cells prints several lines (W4's W-D-03 prints one per `leg_split` cell). A row's verdict is FAIL if any component it evaluates fails, else UNRUN if any required witness or prerequisite is absent (A4r, A11a–c, A12, A0 …), else PASS. So a wrong refusal id is FAIL before the witnesses exist. An expectation's verdict is the worst of its rows (FAIL over UNRUN over PASS); `claims`, completeness, A13's coverage and A14's renderer read that expectation verdict, never a single row.
   - **Pending cells.** A cell whose `[[branch_cell]]` carries `pending = "<item>: <what>"` (A1c; A8b's W-P-01(a) `manual_assertion` cell waits on W5's probe) is still run, and its observed outcome is logged in its step record and printed in its projection (the observed refusal id and exit, or `admit`; A8b's `step1=`). Its line reads `UNRUN <id> … reason=needs:<item> (<what>)` (this bead's rendering of §5.1's `reason=<pending>`), it never counts as PASS, and its authored expected verdict is compared only once the owning item removes `pending`. A FAIL in any other row of the expectation still makes the expectation FAIL. An expectation with a pending cell cannot be claimed: A1c's loader refuses it (`PendingClaimed`). Removing the field, once its probe settles the cell, is the only way to claim the expectation, and a claim never excludes a cell (§5.1; there is no cell-level exclusion, and round 4's proposed `unclaimed_cells` was not adopted).
   - **P rows** (§5.2): first refusal id equal; exit equal to `registries/refusal_catalog.toml`'s (`fa_registry::refusal_catalog::RefusalCatalog`; `FA-REF-008/010/011/012` are `exit = "safety"` (7), `FA-REF-080/086` `exit = "refusal"` (3)); and the refusal names the row's `law_row` (A1c). A `<registry file>#<row key>` row is named by the refusal object's `why` (`{matrix: {state, class}}` for an admissibility cell, `{policy}` or `{law}`) or its cite. A `plan:§<section>` row (a refusal that names only a plan section: the allocation review's `FA-REF-012` cites "plan §14.3 (separation of preparer and approver)", `crates/fa/src/allocation_review/approval.rs:46-53`) passes when the refusal's cite begins `plan §<section>`. Then the structured remedy, one projected token per check, each `ok`, `fail` or `n/a`; `n/a` is printed, never `ok`, and is used only where the check's input cannot exist for that refusal:
     - `matrix=`: `refusal.why.matrix` equals the cell's {state, class}, compared structurally, never as text. Only the period-law and capability sites of the posting pipeline set a matrix (`Why::Matrix` at `crates/fa-journal/src/post.rs:2661`, `:2694`, `:2723`, `:2860` for `PERIOD_LAW` and `:2759` for `CAPABILITY`, at 8196f5e40; `crates/fa-journal/src/refusal.rs:93-100`). So the check is required (`fail` when the matrix is absent or differs) on a row whose `law_row` is an `admissibility.toml` cell and whose refusal is `FA-REF-010` or `FA-REF-011`, and `n/a` on every other row (the SoD denials, the evidence minimum, the approval-count denial `FA-REF-012`, `crates/fa-policy/src/denial.rs:182`, and the allocation review set no matrix);
     - `fix_argv=`: `refusal.fix.argv` parses with `fa_cli::args::parse(argv, flags, switches)` (`crates/fa-cli/src/args.rs:22`) given the named verb's registered flags and switches; `n/a` when `fix.argv` is null, which the refusal object renders when the fix names no parseable `fa` command (`crates/fa-cli/src/refusal_object.rs:19-24`; the allocation review's fix is prose);
     - `alternatives=`: `refusal.alternatives` ⊇ the admissible classes and ops `remedy.rs` derives for that state, on the rows where `matrix=` is required; `n/a` on every other row;
     - `commit_agrees=` (A7c), on the committed representative of each outcome only.

     A refusal with no Appendix D detail (a bare `Refusal::new`, `crates/fa-journal/src/refusal.rs:27-41`, such as W5's route-A cell) therefore prints `matrix=n/a fix_argv=n/a alternatives=n/a` and passes on its id, exit and cite. The reference verdict is A4r's; until it lands that component is UNRUN.
   - **Effective type.** Each line prints the effective type of its (expectation, target), `Case::effective_type(expectation, target)` after A3b's load-time re-types (a per-target K-ORACLE re-type of one M3 target leaves its siblings T), and the completeness rule below reads it.
   - **D rows**: the cites, severity and statistic projected through the fact↔vid map, S/Sd components compared exactly; on a `closed_cites = true` row, every intent that A5b's `unexpected_cites` returns (a documented or Δ entry the run's findings cite and no target names) adds the line `FAIL <id> D <variant> <intent>/<cell> <procedure>@<version> cite=… reason=unexpected finding cites <intent>`, with the unexpected intent in the target slot, which fails the expectation; B, Rel and the pass rule are A5b's (`evaluate_d`); until A5b, a row with a B or Rel component is UNRUN.
8. **Log** every step (§3.5) and every verdict.

**Fact↔vid map.** From fact and intent ids to entry VIds (the `posted`, `recorded` and `batch_posted` events), and from account codes to account VIds, taken from the `plan` event's legs (`account_vid`) and cross-checked against `fa_model::account_vid(book_id, code)` (`crates/fa-model/src/ids.rs:294-298`), which the runner also uses for Rel components (§2.6 rule 9). It projects cites to (fact or intent id, leg ordinal) and account codes (§5.2). Leg ordinals are 0-based: a finding cites a leg by its index in the posted entry (`Cite::Leg { entry, ordinal }`, `crates/fa-lantern/src/lib.rs:359-362`; `ordinal(i)` over the entry's legs, `crates/fa-lantern/src/jet.rs:40-42`, `:105`), `intent_json.rs` renders an intent's legs in `[[leg]] ordinal` order, and the post path keeps that order (no leg sort in fa-journal, fa-model or fa-intents), so the cite `(F4,0)` is the `[[leg]]` with `ordinal = 0`. The runner checks that on every post: the `plan` event's legs must come back in `[[leg]] ordinal` order (their `account_vid`s equal `fa_model::account_vid` of each leg's account, in order), else the case fails before evaluation, naming the intent. `[[value]] ordinal` (A1a) is a separate, 1-based numbering.

**Rows whose data is not committed.** A row whose basis reaches a `rows_file` fact whose file is not committed (OD 2b, §3.2b; A1a's `rows_committed = false`) prints UNRUN `reason=rows file not committed (OD 2b)`; its book is never built from the fetch cache, which tests never read (§4.3).

**Self-disclosure rows (§2.7).** For every book variant the runner generates (`selfrow.rs`) one `jet.self_sourced_evidence` expectation (`registries/procedures.toml:97-105`; `crates/fa-lantern/src/jet.rs:418-516`) with id `<case>-SELF-<variant>`, `<case>` being the case letter (`W-SELF-native`, `E-SELF-statement-bridge`) or the self-test's id (`_selftest-SELF-native`), and `about = "reconstruction"`; case authors never write it (A1c refuses the id). It asserts that the procedure cites every posting that cites a reviewed citation stub, at severity Medium. It is a D about our encoding, not about history, so it is exempt from K-EVID's bar on `statement` and `ledger` variants. It is evaluated like any D row: while a witness it requires is not implemented it prints UNRUN, never PASS. It renders first in "How this book was built" (A14).

**Completeness.** `case.toml` `claims` is the completeness claim: the case's `#[test]` fails when a claimed expectation's verdict (the worst of its rows, step 7) is FAIL or UNRUN, when an unclaimed one is FAIL, and when a claimed one is re-typed (K-TAUT, K-ORACLE or A11b's witness turns a claimed T into R or C): the claim as written did not hold, so the row prints FAIL with its effective type and `reason=retyped T→R <check>`. An unclaimed re-typed row just prints its effective type. No case test carries `#[ignore]` (§16). The runner entry point returns `RunOutcome { lines, log_path, verdicts, ok }`, so the runner's own tests can assert a non-zero outcome without failing themselves. `outcome(case_id)` memoizes each committed case's run in a process-wide `OnceLock` keyed by case, so every `#[test]` of the binary that needs a case's verdicts (the case's own test, A13's closure, A14's drift test) shares one run per `cargo test` invocation, whatever order the test threads run in.

**Planted negatives on a copy (§3.2b).** `run_planted(case_dir, edits: &[PlantedEdit]) -> Result<RunOutcome, RunOutcome>` copies the committed case's embedded files into the test's temp dir, applies the named edits in order (`PlantedEdit { file, old, new }`, where each `old` must occur exactly once in the file as it then stands; a miss panics naming the edit, so a stale plant never silently tests nothing), loads the copy through `CaseFiles` and runs it, and returns `Err(outcome)` when the case verdict is non-zero. Most negatives need one edit; coupled edits (a debit and the credit that keeps its intent balanced, a Δ intent and its step) stay one copy and one test.
- **Where a plant stops.** The copy runs the whole pipeline, load checks first (step 1). A plant that edits a receipt-bound fact value or scope, the `amount_literal` of a `value:` or `derived:` leg, or anything else a load check recomputes stops at load (K-BIND, K-DERIVE: every line reads FAIL with the load failure as its reason, and no book is built), so its observable is that load line. A negative whose observable is a run-time line plants an edit that loads: an `amount_rule = "synthetic"` leg (which no load check recomputes), a step, an expectation field, or a rendered batch. Each negative names which of the two its observable is (A8's F4 debit negative carries one of each). A `<dir>_neg_<slug>` test whose edit must fail the case (a FAIL, or an UNRUN on a claimed row) calls `.expect_err("<what the edit must break>")`; one whose observable leaves the verdict green (a re-type such as `T→R`, or an UNRUN on an unclaimed row) calls `.expect(…)`. Either way it then asserts its exact observable line in `outcome.lines` (a `FAIL <id> …` naming the component, a refusal id and exit, a re-typed line, or an `UNRUN <id> … reason=…`). The committed files are never edited. `neg` is not one of `NEG_NAME_RE`'s tokens (`scripts/gate_evidence_check.sh:151-155`), so the ratchet counts the first kind through the `expect_err(` in its body (`NEG_BODY_RE`), and the second kind only if its slug carries a token such as `fails`, `never`, `wrong`, `missing` or `without`; every slug of the second kind must. A case's negatives run together with `cargo test -p fa-cli --test cases -- <dir>_neg_`.

**Determinism (§3.4).** Book ids are random (`crates/fa-cli/src/cli.rs:3879-3883`), and there is no seed flag. The runner logs the book id and treats VId order as an observed covariate.

## Tests & logging
- **fa-oracles unit tests** (`cargo test -p fa-oracles`): `json_reader_round_trips_robot_lines_and_refuses_floats`; `remedy_checks_are_n_a_without_detail_and_fail_when_a_required_matrix_is_missing` (a recorded refusal object with `why = {cite}` and a null `fix.argv` gives `matrix=n/a fix_argv=n/a alternatives=n/a`; an `FA-REF-010` object on an admissibility row with its matrix removed gives `matrix=fail`); `plan_section_law_row_passes_on_the_refusal_cite_and_fails_on_another_section` (`plan:§14.3` against the allocation review's cite, then against a cite naming §14.4); `observation_parses_posted_plan_error_and_procedure_run_lines` (fixture lines copied from `fa robot-docs examples` output shapes); `step_and_verdict_records_round_trip_through_parse_line`; `stub_bytes_rerender_identically_from_the_sources_row`; `intent_json_matches_the_cli_intent_shape`; `remedy_alternatives_for_original_in_closed_are_derived_from_admissibility`.
- **Runner tests** (`crates/fa-cli/tests/cases.rs`, through the real `fa`; the case negatives are `_selftest_neg_<slug>` tests over `run_planted` copies of `_selftest`):
  - **Positive (§15).** `_selftest` runs every implemented stage, emits one JSONL step record per step whose verb is in `verbs.toml` (`every_logged_verb_is_in_verbs_toml`), and prints one line per evaluated (expectation, variant, target, cell) row, ending with `LOG <path>`.
  - **Negatives (§15):**
    - `_selftest_neg_wrong_refusal_id_reports_fail_with_a_reproducible_argv`: a P row expecting `FA-REF-011` on the Original-in-Closed cell → `FAIL … expected FA-REF-011 observed FA-REF-010`, followed by `REPRODUCE fa --book <dir> post --intent … --json`, and re-running that argv reproduces `FA-REF-010`;
    - `renamed_verb_fails_before_any_book_is_created`: a test-only `HARNESS_VERBS` with `("periods", Some("open"))` → the outcome fails naming the verb, and no book directory was created;
    - `_selftest_neg_approval_row_without_a0_is_unrun_and_fails_the_claimed_case`: a claimed row whose cell has `approval_encoding = entry_approval` → `UNRUN … reason=needs:A0 (fa post --approval)`, `ok = false`;
    - `_selftest_neg_cite_of_the_wrong_fact_reports_fail`: a D target expecting a cite of another fact → `FAIL`;
    - (`renamed_verb_fails_before_any_book_is_created` edits `HARNESS_VERBS`, not a case file, so it stays a plain runner test.)
  - **Derived checks (§2.7, §3.2a, §5.1, §5.2):**
    - `every_variant_gets_exactly_one_self_row`;
    - `expectation_verdict_is_the_worst_of_its_rows` (a runner fixture expectation with one PASS and one UNRUN row reads UNRUN in its verdict records and fails a claim; with a FAIL row, FAIL);
    - `cited_leg_ordinal_is_the_authored_leg_ordinal` (a micro-book intent whose legs 0 and 2 are whole multiples of 10^3: `jet.round_amounts` cites project to `(<id>,0)` and `(<id>,2)`);
    - `period_without_a_period_open_step_stays_future_and_its_post_is_refused` (`FA-REF-010`, observed and reported with its reproduce argv, never repaired);
    - `pre_case_on_a_book_without_a_case_is_the_head_at_the_finding_step` (the logged `--known-at` equals the head after the last history step);
    - `finding_record_step_params_are_passed_as_param_flags` (A1b's `params`; the logged argv carries `--param window_days=4`);
    - `extension_step_kinds_run_through_their_verbs` (a runner fixture with an `allocate` step, an `engagement_file` step with `ref = "finding:<D id>"`, an `event_record` step, `rule_check`/`rule_propose`/`rule_backtest` steps and a `migrate_import` prepare + stage pair: each logs its verb and exit; the backtest's `--compare` equals the digest the propose step's `rule_proposed` event reported, the stage's `--package` equals the oid its prepare reported, and the allocation's rendered `method` equals its `allocation_law`; a planted allocation intent without `allocation_law` is refused before any `fa` invocation);
    - `planted_edit_missing_from_the_copy_is_refused` (an `old` that does not occur panics naming the edit; two coupled edits apply in order);
    - `period_label_map_is_logged_once_per_book`, and `period_label_with_no_matching_period_fails_before_replay`;
    - `computed_account_vid_equals_the_plan_leg_vid`;
    - `manual_assertion_cell_commits_through_attest_manual_plan_out` (the committed entry's proposal is the one the assertion covers; a planted second `fa post --intent` instead is refused by the evidence minimum, which the test observes and names);
    - `unclaimed_unrun_row_does_not_fail_the_case_but_a_claimed_one_does`;
    - `pending_cell_is_run_and_logged_but_never_counted_as_pass` (on a runner fixture with an unclaimed P row, `fixtures::pending_min()`): the pending cell's step record and observed refusal are logged, its line reads `UNRUN … reason=needs:<item> (<what>)`, and a planted wrong refusal id on a sibling cell of the same row turns the row FAIL.
- **Output (§16).**
  - Per evaluated (expectation, variant, target, cell) row (step 7): `PASS|FAIL|UNRUN <id> <type> <variant> <target>/<cell> <projection-with-statuses> [reason=<text>]`. Field order is §16's; this bead owns the in-field serialization, and the case beads' expected lines use it: `<variant>` is the book stem; `<target>` is the target fact id, or `-` for a row with no target (T/R reads, generated rows); `<cell>` is `default` or `attr=value[,attr=value]` with §2.6's attribute names; each projected component is `<name>=<status>:<value>` (D: `<procedure>@<version> cite=… severity=… statistic=…`; P: `refusal=<FA-REF-NNN> exit=<n> law=<row> ref=<agree|disagree|not_modelled> matrix=<ok|fail|n/a> fix_argv=<ok|fail|n/a> alternatives=<ok|fail|n/a>`, plus `commit_agrees=<ok|fail>` on a committed representative (A7c); N: `kind=… twin=…`; T/R/C: `kind=… read=<verb>,<line|account>,<coordinate>[,<selector>] value=<exact> published=<exact>` (R: `posted=<exact>`) plus the witness tokens it needs, for example `read=statement balance-sheet,assets,2000Q4,as-originally-reported`): `contrib=holds|retyped(<condition>)` (A11b), `outsider=<read kind>:verified|failed` (A12's table of read kinds: `report`, `replay`, `replay+bundle`; a read verb outside that table leaves its T UNRUN `reason=outsider`), `kernel=ok|fail` (A7b's kernel twin, where it applies), `append_only=ok|fail` (A12, a restatement-case T) and, on every T and R row, `gql=ok|fail|not_run(pending holds)` (A12's second read path; not a T witness, so `not_run` never turns a row UNRUN). The VerdictRecord's `witnesses` carries each witness's verdict, the named-input ids A11b checked and the periods its reads covered. The selector slot is `as-originally-reported`, `as-currently-stated` or `as-reported-in=<placeholder>` (A7b's `$C_ISSUE_<period>[_<n>]`), rendered from A1c's `read_selector` (`originally_reported`, `currently_stated`, `reported_in:<period label>[#<n>]`); a case bead's `sel=<selector>` spelling (E4) means this slot, and no line carries a separate `sel=` token. The case beads' expected lines fix each field's contents; some spell a read as `verb(args)`, which the runner prints in this comma form. `law=` prints the expectation's authored `law_row` (A1c's `<registry file>#<row key>`, or `plan:§<section>`, which has no space so the token stays one field; W5's proposed `cite:plan §14.3` is this form). `reason=` comes last and only on FAIL or UNRUN: `needs:<item> (<what>)` for a missing prerequisite (`needs:A0 (fa post --approval)`), the witness's name for a missing witness (`filler_invariance`, `contributing_entry`, `mutation_twin`, `kernel_twin`, `outsider`, `reference`; several missing witnesses are comma-joined in that order, `reason=contributing_entry,outsider`), and `expected <x> observed <y>` for a FAIL. The plan does not fix the reason token; round 2's `-- <reason>` form is replaced by `reason=`, which the case beads already use.
  - On failure: `REPRODUCE fa --book <retained dir> <exact argv>` for the failing step.
  - At the end: `LOG <jsonl path>`.
- **JSONL log (§3.5).** One `StepRecord` per step: `{record:"step", case, book_variant, branch_class, filler_variant, book_id, step, verb, argv_redacted, exit, refusal_id?, finding_procedure?, certificate_kind?, elapsed_ms, binds}`. `argv_redacted` replaces every run-variant token by a placeholder (`$BOOK` for the book dir, `$<fact id>` for an entry vid, `$C_<KIND>_<period>` for a certificate, `$APPROVAL_<n>`, `$DOC_<document id>`), and `binds` maps each placeholder to the event and field that produced it (`$F4` → `posted.vid` of step 12), which A14b's transcript mode replays. One `VerdictRecord` per printed row (step 7), from which the expectation's verdict is derived. stdout is data and stderr is diagnostics. The log goes to the test's temp dir and, when `FA_CASES_LOG_DIR` is set, is copied there as `<dir>.jsonl` (`worldcom.jsonl`) for the renderer.

## Notes
- **Dependencies.** A1b (book variants), A1c (expectations), A3a (load-time checks the pipeline's step 1 runs).
- **Plan vs code, the structured remedy.** §5.2 lists `why.matrix`, `fix.argv` and `alternatives` for every P row. At 8196f5e40 only `FA-REF-010` and `FA-REF-011` from the posting pipeline carry a matrix (step 7), and a refusal whose fix names no `fa` command carries a null `fix.argv`, so those checks print `n/a` where their input cannot exist and stay required where it must. A case line that prints `matrix=ok` on an SoD or evidence-minimum row (W4's and W5's `FA-REF-008` W-P-01 cells, for example) reads `matrix=n/a` under this rule.
- **Plan vs bead, the log.** §3.5 lists step fields only. A13 ("passed in the last run") and A14 (render "from the last green run's JSONL log") read expectation verdicts from the log, so the verdict record is added; A14b needs `binds` to rebind placeholders.
- **Period codes.** Checked at 8196f5e40: `fa period create` assigns period codes itself (`FY<N>-P<nn>` plus `-OPEN`, `-A<k>` and `-CLOSE`, `crates/fa-cli/tests/cli.rs:15494-15530`), which is why §3.2a makes `[[period]] code` a label mapped at run time.
- **Approvals and fa-z46b.** Today a second principal is a second `--as` label from the runner's own process. Under fa-z46b that will no longer satisfy dual control, so all approval production stays in `approve_as` (see A7b).
- **Stub documents.** §15 A7a builds the citation-stub documents of §2.7: the renderer and the `fa document add` / `attest review` steps. Placeholders (`fa:case-placeholder:v1`, the §2.6 `evidence_attached` cells) are branch cells evaluated through A7c, and the filler document (`fa:case-filler:v1`) is A6's.
- **Later pipeline steps.** Issuance, cases and certified reads are A7b's; dry-run sweeps are A7c's; migration is A7m's; the witnesses are A11a–c; outsider verification and `fa verify book` are A8a/A12 (§3.3 steps 8–9). Their rows print UNRUN until those items land.
- **Budget.** Runtime under 60 s per case on a warm worker (§16). A7c measures it.
- **fa-81lu (AttachEvidence and provenance grades, in progress, LavenderSwan)** landed `jet.self_sourced_evidence` (15668e55, per its 2026-10-23 comment), the procedure the generated `<case>-SELF-<variant>` rows run. Its open work (ThirdPartySigned and Connector grades, grades in bundles) can change what the citation stubs (graded by their uploader, `AgentUpload`) and the SELF rows' findings read; a change there is re-observed in those rows, never regenerated. No bead plans the case runner itself.
- **Case-local names this bead serves** (from the case beads): `<dir>_neg_<slug>` negatives, the expected-line notation above, `reason=`, step principals such as W3's `case-closer-1`, `[[ledger_map]]` batches (M0), and M0's `selftest`/`selftest_neg_ledger_` test filters (which match `_selftest` and `_selftest_neg_ledger_…`).
- **Landing gates.** `bash scripts/tier0.sh` before main, `cargo test -p fa-oracles -p fa-cli --test cases`; `bash scripts/check.sh` for the `_selftest` data and the `Cargo.toml` edit.
- **No registry edit.** A7a adds a dev-dependency (`Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock` change: a global crate-verdict input, `scripts/crate_verdicts.py:13-15`), which the landing states.

