description:
## Why
Plan: `docs/FORENSIC_CASE_STUDIES_PLAN.md` §15 A1c, §5.1–§5.4, §0.3.

The expectations file is where every claim of the program is declared, so its schema is where the claim types of §0.3 (D, P, T, C, R, N and their CF rows) become checkable data. Every sentence is generated from a template id and never hand-written (§5.1 `statement_template`; §12.3 "There is no free-text path"). Per-entry detail goes into `[[target]]` rows and branch tables into `[[branch_cell]]` rows, because the TOML subset cannot nest (§3.1). A published observation that franken_accounting does not compute is a `[[note]]`. A note has no verdict, is never PASS or FAIL, renders only in Limits, and is never counted as an expectation (§5.1; W-NOTE-01 replaced the v0 W-T-01 for exactly this reason, §6.4, Appendix Z.7).

## What to build
A loader and validator for `crates/fa-oracles/cases/<dir>/expectations.toml`.

**Files and public API** (introduced here):
- `crates/fa-oracles/src/cases/expect.rs`: `pub struct Expectation`, `pub enum ExpectationType { D, P, T, C, R, N, CF }`, `pub struct Target`, `pub struct BranchCell`, `pub struct Note`, `pub enum Status { S, Sd, Si, B(Vec<Attribute>), Rel(Covariate), X }`, `pub struct Component { status, value }`, `pub struct Projection` (the §5.4 tuple), and `pub fn load_expectations(case: &Case, text: &str) -> Result<Expectations, Vec<LoadError>>`. `Case` gains `expectations`.
- `crates/fa-oracles/src/cases/templates.rs`: `pub const TEMPLATE_IDS`, the closed list of `statement_template` ids. The plan names the §0.3 templates without ids, so A1c fixes them: `D.detection`, `D.restates_characterization` (§2.6 rule 4: a D whose qualitative constraint alone collapses the predicate "renders as 'restates the source's characterization'"; never counted as a finding), `P.first_refusal`, `T.bridge`, `T.footing`, `T.per_account`, `T.allocation`, `T.delta`, `T.rule` (M-T-02, below), `T.round_trip` (X-T-02, below), `C.band`, `R.read_back`, `N.no_refusal`, `N.no_finding`, `N.exact_gap`, `CF.counterfactual`, `NOTE.observation`. Each type accepts only its own prefix. The template texts are the renderer's (A14).
- New `LoadErrorKind`s: `BadId`, `ReservedId`, `ExcludedField`, `NoteWithVerdict`, `ExitIsCatalogs`, `AllComponentsX`, `PendingClaimed`, `ReadFieldsTwice`.

**`[[expectation]]` common fields (§5.1).** `id`, `type`, `book_variant`, `statement_template`, `basis` (fact ids), `premises`, `granularity`, `capabilities`, `owner_review`, `about` (`history` default / `reconstruction`), `record_label` (`characterized` default / `silent`), `known_at` (`pre_case` default for D on `native`; `head` for W-D-07/W-D-08 only). `pre_case` is the recorded head just before the variant's first `case_open` step; on a variant with no `[[case]]` row (W4's migration book after `fa migrate finalize`, M5's and M7's ledger book after its last batch) it is the head at the `finding_record` step itself. A1c resolves it to a step position (`KnownAt::frontier(book, step)`, wired with the book keys below); the runner (A7a) passes the recorded sequence it observed there as `--known-at`.

**Id grammar.** `<case>-D-NN`, `-P-NN`, `-T-NN`, `-C-NN`, `-R-NN`, `-N-NN` (NN two digits); CF rows `<parent>-CF-NN`, numbered per parent from 01; notes `<case>-NOTE-NN`. `<case>` is the case's `letter` from `case.toml` (§3.2b: `W-D-01`, `W-NOTE-01`), or, for a `harness_selftest` case, which has no letter, its id (`_selftest-T-01`; A1a). Three id forms are reserved and refused when authored (`ReservedId`): `<case>-SELF-<variant>`, which the runner generates for every book variant (§2.7, A7a); `<case>-CASE-<case id>`, the `fa case show` read-back the runner generates for every restatement case (§3.3 step 5, A7b); and `<case>-N-90`, the guidance-reachability N that A16's probe generates for every case (§12.5).

**Type-specific fields** (flattened; one-line arrays where a field is a list):
- **D** (§5.2): `procedure`, `version`. Each `[[target]]` carries the components `cite` (one-line array of `<subject>#<leg ordinal>` and account codes; the subject is a fact id or an `[[intent]]` id of the variant, such as W-D-08's `delta-F4`, and the leg ordinal is 0-based, the kernel's index of the leg in its entry, `crates/fa-lantern/src/jet.rs:40-42`), `severity`, `statistic_name`, `statistic_value`, `statistic_scale`, each with its status field (`cite_status`, `severity_status`, `statistic_status`): `S`, `Sd`, `Si`, `B(<attr>,…)`, `Rel(account_vid_order)` or `X`.
- **P** (§5.2): `intent` (fact id), `refusal`, `law_row`, `attribute`, `reference_verdict` (a refusal id, `admit`, or `not_modelled`). The expected exit is not a field: it is read from `registries/refusal_catalog.toml`, and a P row carrying `exit` is refused (`ExitIsCatalogs`). `law_row` has two forms (§5.2 after polish round 4):
  - `<registry file>#<row key>`, for example `admissibility.toml#Original/Closed` or `sod_policy.toml#draft-promote`;
  - `plan:§<section>`, where the refusal names only a plan section. The allocation review's `FA-REF-012` (`fa allocate approve` by the preparer) cites "plan §14.3 (separation of preparer and approver)" (`crates/fa/src/allocation_review/approval.rs:51`), and its refusal object then carries `why = {cite: <that prose>}` (`crates/fa-cli/src/refusal_object.rs:16-18`, `:184-188`; current tree). So W5's route-A cell is `law_row = "plan:§14.3"`, and the runner compares it with the envelope's `why.cite`, which must begin `plan §14.3`. The form has no space, so the printed `law=plan:§14.3` stays one line field; W5's proposed `cite:plan §14.3` is this form.

  A `law_row` in neither form is refused (`WrongType`).
- **N** (§5.2): `kind` (`no_refusal` | `no_finding` | `exact_gap`), the target, `procedure` and `version` (required on `no_finding` and `exact_gap`, whose procedure reports or computes on the target; refused on `no_refusal`, which observes the kernel's admission; E6's E-N-03 is a `no_finding` N), `mechanism` (`<path>:<line>` at the pinned commit), and its twin: `twin_kind` (`mutation` | `harness_observability` | `matcher`, §3.7 item 3; E-N-01 and L-N-01 are harness-observability twins, A16's guidance N a matcher twin), `twin_attribute`, `twin_value`, `twin_expect` (`reports`, `refuses <FA-REF-NNN>` or `gap_zero`). An `exact_gap` N (M-N-01) also states `gap_amount`, `gap_unit` and `twin_target` (a target without the mechanism's input, where the gap must be 0).
- **CF** (§5.2): `parent`, `changed_attribute`, `changed_value` (exactly one attribute), and the expected verdict.
- **T** (§5.3): `read_verb`, `read_target` (line or account), `read_coordinate`, `read_selector`, `published` (fact id), `kind` (`bridge` / `footing` / `per_account` / `allocation` / `delta` / `rule` / `round_trip`), `vintages` (a one-line array of `<role>:<source id>`, roles `A`, `D`, `B` for a bridge, `exhibit` for a footing, `source` for a per-account, allocation or `rule` value, `D` alone for a delta, `B` for a round trip; K-VINTAGE, A3b, reads the roles), `assurance` (the T's own assurance status, §5.3 and §10.1: X-T-01's quarterly lines are `filer_unaudited` inside an audited 10-K/A; A3c's K-ASSERT renders it in place of the source row's status), `inputs_named` (fact ids or `[[intent]]` ids of the variant, resolved as in the foreign keys below; W-T-03 names `delta-F12` and `delta-F13`).
  - `read_selector` is empty or one of `originally_reported`, `currently_stated` and `reported_in:<period label>[#<n>]`, the n-th `issue` step of that period in step order (default 1); E4's E-T-04 reads the bridge book's second FY1999 issuance, the 8-K's, as `reported_in:<its FY1999 label>#2`. The runner prints it in A7a's read slot (`as-originally-reported`, `as-currently-stated`, `as-reported-in=$C_ISSUE_<period>[_<n>]`).
  - Two case-local kinds extend §5.3's five. `rule` is M-T-02's "T over the interpretive rule" (§9.3): a registered derivation, named in the field `rule` (an A2 id such as `trustee_transfer_credit`), applied to a certified read, whose output must equal the published value (M3). `round_trip` is X-T-02's narrow export round trip (§10.1): the as-restated instant totals `fa export xbrl` writes, compared by concept QName and value with the instance's tagged B, which the plan types T but §5.3 lists no kind for (X4). Its outsider route is A12's.
- **C** (§5.3): `display_unit` (the fact stating the display unit or rounding note), `per_row_test`, `band`, `detection_limit`.
- **R**: the read fields and `posted` (the intent or fact whose posted value it reads back).
- **Where T, C and R fields sit.** `kind`, `vintages`, `assurance`, `inputs_named`, `rule` and C's band fields sit on the `[[expectation]]`. The read fields (`read_verb`, `read_target`, `read_coordinate`, `read_selector`) and `published` (R: `posted`) sit either on the expectation, for a single read (its lines print target `-`), or on each of its `[[target]]` rows, for several reads (X5's one target per complete group, W7's W-R-04 one per moved cell, M3's one per account; lines print the target's `fact`), never both (`ReadFieldsTwice`). A3b's per-target re-types and A11b's witness follow the same placement.

**`[[target]]`** (§5.1): `expectation`, `fact` (a fact id, or an `[[intent]]` id of the variant for a D target whose subject is a Δ or derived posting, W-D-08's `delta-F4`), `basis`, `branch_scope`, `branch_attrs` (one-line array of the attributes its cells vary), `admitted_cell` (for D), the component fields above. **`[[branch_cell]]`** (§5.1): `expectation`, `target`, `cell` (a one-line array aligned with the target's `branch_attrs`, one value per attribute, each value opaque to this loader: A5a fixes the spellings, for example F4's per-leg `leg_split` values `mmm` … `nnn`, one letter per leg in 0-based ordinal order), the expected verdict, `record_consistent`, `default`, and the optional `pending` (below). A5a's enumerator emits cells in exactly this key form. **`[[note]]`** (§5.1): `id`, `case`, `basis` (fact ids), `template` (`NOTE.observation`). A note that names a verdict field (`verdict`, `refusal`, `procedure`, any component) is refused (`NoteWithVerdict`).

**Foreign keys.**
- `book_variant` → a `books/<stem>.toml` file among the case's files, the stem `<kind>[-<suffix>]` (§3.2b; a presence check that needs no book parser);
- `basis`, `twin_target`, `published` → fact ids (A1a);
- `[[target]] fact`, `inputs_named` entries and `cite` subjects → a fact id, or an `[[intent]]` id of the expectation's variant (the intent half needs a parsed book and is wired with the keys below). §3.2b makes a documented entry's intent id its fact id, so the two coincide there; a Δ `delta-<target>[-<n>]` or a derived posting is an intent id only;
- `[[target]] expectation`, `[[branch_cell]] target`, CF `parent` → their rows;
- D `procedure`@`version` → a row of `registries/procedures.toml` at that version (`fa_registry::procedures::Procedures::embedded()`, `get`, `crates/fa-registry/src/procedures.rs:184`, `:331`);
- P `refusal` and `twin_expect`'s refusal → a code in `registries/refusal_catalog.toml` (`fa_registry::refusal_catalog::RefusalCatalog::embedded()`, `by_code`, `refusal_catalog.rs:64`, `:141`);
- P `intent` → a fact with an `[[intent]]` in the named variant;
- the books' step refs that name expectations (A1b's table): `finding_review` → an expectation id; `assert_balance` → `<expectation id>[#<target fact>]`, a T or R whose read fields give the account and period, and with `#<target fact>` one of its `[[target]]` rows whose `fact` is that id (W7's W-R-04, one per (account, quarter) cell; a row whose reads sit on targets needs the `#` part); `engagement_file` `finding:<expectation id>` → a D expectation of the same variant whose `procedure`@`version` has an earlier `finding_record` step there (W3, W4, W8). The step's `asserts` fact (A1b) is A1b's own key;
- `read_selector = reported_in:<label>[#<n>]` → an `issue` step of the variant whose `ref` is that `[[period]]` label (A1b's `issue` ref), the n-th such step in step order; A3b's K-VINTAGE then reads that step's filing-stub `document` (A1b);
- `case.toml` `claims` → an authored expectation id, or a runner-generated row of this case: `<case>-SELF-<variant>` for a declared variant stem, `<case>-CASE-<case id>` for a `[[case]]` row of some variant, and `<case>-N-90` (A8 claims `W-SELF-native`; W6 and X7 claim their `<letter>-CASE-*` rows). Anything else is `DanglingKey`. `claims` is the completeness claim the runner enforces (A7a): an UNRUN or FAIL on a claimed expectation fails the case's `#[test]`, while an unclaimed row (a walking-skeleton row whose witness has not landed) prints its verdict and fails the test only on FAIL (§15 rules, §16).

**Optional fields later items add** (accepted once their item lands; each item extends this loader's field table): `population` and `population_basis` on an `[[expectation]]` (A4's sourced-and-complete marker for a P- or A-tier D or N); `constraints`, `exceptions` and `rel_rule` on a `[[target]]`, and `closed_cites` on a D `[[expectation]]` (A5b); `owner_approved` on an `[[expectation]]` (A14; only with `owner_review = true`).

**An all-X row is a note.** A D or P expectation every one of whose targets has every component status X is refused (`AllComponentsX`): it can neither PASS nor FAIL, so it is a `[[note]]` (§6.4: W-D-05 became the Limits note W-NOTE-02 for this reason).

**Record consistency.** `[[branch_cell]] record_consistent = false` is a legal, evaluated cell (W-P-03's Locked column and W-P-04 are such cells, §6.3, §6.4); A5b decides which cells must carry it.

**Pending cells (§5.1).** `[[branch_cell]] pending` is empty, or `"<item id>: <what it settles>"`: the cell's verdict waits on a probe or product item that another §15 item owns. The first user is A8b's W-P-01(a) `manual_assertion` cell, which waits on W5's probe of whether a ManualAssertion can cover an intent planned into a draft (§15 W5; PLAN_DELTA after polish round 3); W5's own probe cells use the same field. The runner (A7a) still runs the cell and logs its observation, and prints it `UNRUN … reason=needs:<item> (<what>)` (A7a's rendering of §5.1's `reason=<pending>`) with the observed outcome; the cell keeps its authored expected verdict. **An expectation with a pending cell cannot be claimed:** the loader refuses a `pending` cell on an expectation listed in `case.toml` `claims` (`PendingClaimed`). Removing the field, once the owning item's probe settles the cell, is the only way to claim the expectation, and a claim never excludes a cell: there is no cell-level carve-out from `claims`.

**Projection fields (§5.4).** Expected values are stored as the verdict projection: (procedure|law id, version, cited fact ids, severity|refusal id + exit, statistic name, statistic value, statistic scale), each component with its status. Fields the projection always excludes cannot carry an expected value (`ExcludedField`): `statistic_population`, `findings_digest`, finding and certificate ids, and any value that depends on storage identifiers (a key ending `_vid`, `_oid` or `_digest`).

## Tests & logging
Unit tests in `crates/fa-oracles/src/cases/expect.rs`, run by `cargo test -p fa-oracles`; negative names follow `scripts/gate_evidence_check.sh:151-155`.
- **Positive.** `pilot_expectations_load_and_every_key_resolves`: `crates/fa-oracles/tests/fixtures/pilot/expectations.toml` (A1c authors it from §6.4 and §15 A8: W-D-01 on F4 and F9, W-D-03 on F4, W-N-01 on F2, W-P-02's no-evidence branch on F4, W-P-03's Original-in-Closed cell) loads with the pilot book, every foreign key resolves, and its canonical bytes round-trip.
- **Negatives** ("Acceptance as A1b"), each a planted edit asserting the kind and the line:
  - `target_with_unknown_expectation_is_refused` (`expectation = "W-D-99"` → `DanglingKey`);
  - `branch_cell_with_unknown_target_is_refused`;
  - `expectation_with_unknown_book_variant_is_refused` (`book_variant = "statment"`);
  - the item's own: `note_naming_a_verdict_is_refused` (a W-NOTE-01 copy with `verdict = "PASS"` → `NoteWithVerdict`).
- **Derived negatives** (from §5.1, §5.2, §5.4, §2.7, §12.5):
  - `p_row_with_exit_field_is_refused` (`exit = 3` → `ExitIsCatalogs`);
  - `expected_value_on_excluded_field_is_refused` (`statistic_population = 12` → `ExcludedField`);
  - `exact_gap_without_gap_amount_or_twin_target_is_refused`, and `n_kind_outside_the_closed_set_is_refused` (`kind = "no_alarm"`);
  - `cf_row_changing_two_attributes_is_refused` (the shape half; A5b checks the semantics);
  - `authored_self_row_is_refused` (`W-SELF-native` → `ReservedId`) and `authored_guidance_n_is_refused` (`W-N-90` → `ReservedId`);
  - `id_with_wrong_case_prefix_is_refused` (`E-D-01` inside case `worldcom`, letter `W` → `BadId`), and `authored_case_show_row_is_refused` (`W-CASE-wc-restatement` → `ReservedId`);
  - `all_x_expectation_is_refused` (a copy of W-NOTE-02's `jet.post_closing` row authored as a D with every status X → `AllComponentsX`);
  - `t_rule_kind_without_its_rule_is_refused` (`kind = "rule"` with no `rule` field → `MissingField`), with the positive `t_rule_kind_names_a_registered_derivation`;
  - `unknown_template_id_is_refused`, and a D row naming `P.first_refusal` is refused;
  - `claimed_expectation_with_a_pending_cell_is_refused` (W-P-01 in `claims` with its `manual_assertion` cell `pending` → `PendingClaimed`), with the positive `pending_cell_on_an_unclaimed_expectation_loads`, and `claimed_expectation_loads_once_pending_is_removed` (the same copy with the field deleted);
  - `law_row_in_neither_form_is_refused` (`law_row = "plan §14.3"`, with a space → `WrongType`), with the positive `law_row_naming_a_plan_section_loads` (`plan:§14.3` on a route-A-shaped P row);
  - `no_finding_n_without_its_procedure_is_refused` (`MissingField`) and `no_refusal_n_naming_a_procedure_is_refused` (`UnknownField`);
  - `t_read_fields_on_expectation_and_target_both_are_refused` (`ReadFieldsTwice`), with the positive `multi_read_t_with_read_fields_on_each_target_loads`;
  - `t_round_trip_kind_loads_with_its_template` (positive), and `round_trip_named_with_another_template_is_refused` (a `round_trip` T naming `T.bridge`);
  - `inputs_named_entry_naming_neither_a_fact_nor_an_intent_is_refused` (`DanglingKey`), with the positive `inputs_named_and_d_target_may_name_a_delta_intent` (`delta-F12` in `inputs_named`, a D target `fact = "delta-F4"` with `cite = ["delta-F4#0"]`);
  - `reported_in_selector_with_no_such_issue_step_is_refused` (`reported_in:FY1999#2` on a variant with one FY1999 `issue` step → `DanglingKey`);
  - `assert_balance_ref_naming_an_unknown_target_is_refused` (`ref = "W-R-04#F99"` → `DanglingKey`) and `assert_balance_ref_naming_a_d_expectation_is_refused` (`WrongType`), with the positive `assert_balance_ref_with_a_target_resolves`;
  - `engagement_file_finding_ref_naming_a_non_d_expectation_is_refused` (`finding:W-P-03` → `WrongType`) and `engagement_file_finding_ref_without_an_earlier_finding_record_step_is_refused` (`DanglingKey`);
  - `claims_naming_an_unknown_id_is_refused` (`DanglingKey`), with the positive `claims_may_name_generated_self_case_and_guidance_rows` (`W-SELF-native`, `W-CASE-<case id>` of a declared `[[case]]`, `W-N-90`);
  - `known_at_pre_case_resolves_before_case_open_or_at_the_step` (positive, on two fixture books: one with a `case_open` step, where the frontier is the step before it, and one with no `[[case]]`, where it is the `finding_record` step's own position).
- **Logging.** Loader errors print `<file>:<line>: <kind>: <detail>` with the row id. There are no runner lines.

## Notes
- **Dependency.** A1c ← A1a: the loader plumbing, `row_line`, the shared pilot fixture and the case-id rule. `book_variant` resolves by file presence, so A1c needs no book parser. The keys that need a parsed book (P `intent` → an `[[intent]]` of the variant; the books' `assert_balance` and `finding_review` refs → expectations; the intent half of `[[target]] fact`, `inputs_named` and `cite`; `reported_in` → an `issue` step; `assert_balance` `#<target fact>` and `engagement_file` `finding:` refs; `claims` → a `[[case]]` row's `<case>-CASE-<case id>`; `KnownAt::frontier`) are wired by whichever of A1b and A1c lands second, with their negatives (both depend only on A1a, §15).
- **The `cell` field.** "One value per branched attribute" is flattened as the one-line array aligned with `branch_attrs`; A5a's enumerator must produce the same keys (its cell-key test reads this loader).
- **Template ids.** The ids above are A1c's choice; A3c (K-ASSERT/K-ERRRATE render per template) and A14 (template texts) use them.
- **Reserved ids.** `<case>-SELF-<variant>` comes from §2.7 and A7a; `<case>-CASE-<case id>` from §3.3 step 5 and A7b; `<case>-N-90` from §12.5 and A16. All three are generated, never authored, so a case author cannot shadow them.
- **Case-local names this bead accepts:** the `rule` T kind (M3's M-T-02) and the `round_trip` T kind (X4's X-T-02, which §10.1 types T and §5.3's five kinds do not cover); stems with a suffix (§3.2b). The `[[target]]` spellings the case items use for sub-rows (W6's `asfiled_R`, `drift_R`) are target names, which the loader treats as opaque unique keys within their expectation. E3/E3b's expected lines spell an evidence-minimum row `evidence_policy.toml[[minimum]](Original)`; in this bead's `law_row` grammar that is `evidence_policy.toml#Original` (the file's `[[minimum]]` rows are keyed by class), and A7a prints the authored form.
- **Pending cells (polish rounds 4 and 5).** The plan has cell-level UNRUNs that wait on another item's probe (§15 W5: W-P-01(a)'s `manual_assertion` cell, and W-P-01(b)'s route-B cells if they do not reach `FA-REF-008`); `pending` is the field that says which cell waits on what (§5.1 now names it). A7a reads it, A8b and W5 author it. Since polish round 5 the rule is strict: an expectation with a pending cell is never in `claims` (`PendingClaimed`), so a claimed row that waits on a probe is not claimable until the probe item removes the field.
- **Plan vs bead (polish round 5).**
  - `law_row`'s plan-section form is §5.2's since polish round 4; its spelling `plan:§<section>` is this bead's.
  - §5.2 lists no `procedure` on an N, but a `no_finding` or `exact_gap` N runs one, and A4's K-TIER, A11c's tier-P bar and A3c's evidence-reading rule read it (E6's E-N-03 needs it).
  - §5.1 reserves `known_at = head` for W-D-07/W-D-08 and makes `pre_case` the D default on `native`, but defines `pre_case` only relative to a case; the case-less reading above is this bead's (W4, M5 and M7 need it).
  - `claims` naming generated ids is the generated-id half of round 4's claims-granularity item (A8, A8a and the case beads already claim them); the cell half is settled by the strict `pending` rule.
  - Read-field placement and intent ids in `inputs_named`, `[[target]] fact` and `cite` are this bead's (W7's W-T-03 and W-D-08, X5, M3); A3b's K-ORACLE and K-TAUT re-type per target where the reads sit on targets.
  - `inputs_named` is K-ORACLE's declaration (which `oracle_output` inputs the T names), not the contributing-entry witness's named-input set. A11b computes that set itself: the intents of the T's `basis` facts except `published`, plus the `inputs_named` ids, plus their Δ intents (a Δ-column read counts the Δs only).
- **Landing gates.** `bash scripts/tier0.sh` before main, `cargo test -p fa-oracles`; `bash scripts/check.sh` for the fixture file.
- **The worked example.** §3.2a's worked example for the A8 pilot is authored as the shared A1a–A1c acceptance fixture (the plan does not reproduce it). A1c authors its `expectations.toml` from §6.4 and §15 A8.
- **No registry edit.** A1c reads `procedures.toml` and `refusal_catalog.toml` through fa-registry and edits neither.

