- the cell/CF verdict field is `expect`: admit or a catalogued refusal on P, finding|no_finding on D, either on N;
- the D component fields are cite/severity/statistic_* with *_status;
- a rule T resolves against §2.4's derivation ids (DERIVATION_IDS) until A2's case_derivations.toml exists. A2 should replace that constant with the table lookup.
Not covered: the "accepted once their item lands" fields (population, constraints, rel_rule, closed_cites, owner_approved) are refused today, as the bead says.
In progress for an independent verifier.

description:
## Why
BLMIS facts are `ledger_row` granularity: 3,793 printed rows (docs/FORENSIC_CASE_STUDIES_PLAN.md §9.2). They are represented by reference, as one `[[fact]]` per complaint pointing at a generated rows file, not as 3,793 hand-written facts (§15 M2t).

Hand-transcription would reintroduce exactly the research-record errors the program guards against (§4.5). So a tool extracts the rows:
- under declared per-complaint layouts;
- with structural checks that FAIL;
- with arithmetic diagnostics that only log, because the T/C classification is the case's, made from §9.2, not the extractor's.

It serves the BLMIS T, C, D and N rows (M3–M7).

## What to build
**`tools/case-fetch/case_fetch.py exhibit-b <case>`** (§4.4; Python 3 stdlib only, run with `python3 -B`; it reads the extraction `extract` left in the cache):
- **Output.** `crates/fa-oracles/cases/blmis/exhibit_b_rows.toml`, GENERATED and never hand-edited, like `receipts.toml`, written in a fixed order (complaint, account, page, line) so a re-run is byte-identical. One `[[row]]` per printed Exhibit B row, every field a scalar or a one-line array of basic strings (the `fa_registry::toml` subset, `crates/fa-registry/src/toml.rs:1-9`):
  - `complaint` (source id), `account` (trustee id), `page`, `line` (in the extraction), `date` (`"YYYY-MM-DD"`, a string, never a TOML date);
  - `transaction_description` (a transaction-type token, below);
  - `amount_literal`, and `column`: the printed header of the principal-movement column it was printed in;
  - `star` (true for Square One's `*` cells);
  - two fields beyond §15 M2t's list, needed by M-T-01's per-row comparison and by the structural column check: `balance_literal` (the row's printed "Balance of Principal" cell) and `cells` (every printed cell of the row, in layout order, as a one-line array; empty string for a blank cell).

  `facts.toml` declares one `[[fact]]` per complaint that points at this file (M2a–M2c).
- **The rows-block hash.** Each complaint's rows are written as one contiguous block (every `[[row]]` of that complaint, in the fixed order, nothing interleaved). The complaint rows fact's `quote_sha256` (M2a–M2c) is `rows_sha256(complaint)` = the SHA-256 of that block's exact bytes, from its first `[[row]]` header line through the newline ending its last field line. The tool prints it per complaint (`ROWS <complaint> rows=<n> sha256=<hex>`), `verify` writes it into the rows fact's receipt (this item extends A9's `verify`, which locates one quote per fact, with that `rows_file` case: `quote_sha256` from the block, and M2a's receipt booleans checked row by row against the extraction), and fa-oracles recomputes it over the same text slice (K-BIND), so Python and Rust hash identical bytes without either porting the other's TOML canonicalization.
- **Layouts.** Per complaint: Glantz 9 columns; Square One 12 (including its 6-year column); Magnify 11 (the SRC-BLM-* rows, M1).
  - A `LAYOUTS` table in the tool names each column, in printed order, by the complaint's own column header (the header strings M1 receipts), and maps it to a row field.
  - The tool never infers a layout: a complaint id with no `LAYOUTS` entry, or a header line that differs from the declared headers, is a FAIL.
- **Structural checks (FAIL on mismatch):**
  - every extracted row has exactly its layout's column count;
  - every amount parses under the literal rule (A9's). Exhibit B prints negatives in parentheses, e.g. the originating principal `(4,854,688)` (§9.2), so this item declares the parenthesized form as negative and tests it;
  - the row count per account equals the count of dated lines on that account's pages of the extraction.
- **Arithmetic diagnostics (logged to stderr, never FAIL):**
  - the pure running sum of each account's principal-movement amounts against its printed balance column: rows off, maximum gap, closing gap;
  - the column sums against any printed footer (Glantz's Deposits footer, for example).

  A footer or balance disagreement is evidence for C, not an extraction error.
- **`*` cells.** Square One's 709 cells marked `*` under footnote [1] (SRC-BLM-005) are extracted with `star = true` and their printed amount. An expectation that reads a starred cell names the footnote in its premises.
- **Descriptions (K-NAMES, §9.4).** Row descriptions are encoded only as transaction-type tokens and trustee account ids, so no account holder's name enters the rows file. The token table is declared in the tool from the descriptions the three complaints print (for example `CHECK`, `STOP PAYMENT`, `TRANS` with its counterpart trustee account id); an unmapped description is a FAIL, never passed through.
- **Gating (OD 2b; §3.2b).** The rows file is generated in the cache (`$FA_CASES_CACHE`). Committing it requires OD 2b. Tests never read the cache (§4.3), so until then every expectation built on the rows prints `UNRUN <id> … reason=rows file not committed (OD 2b)` (M0's pending-rows rule), and the extractor is built and tested on the fixtures below.
- **Exit codes** follow A9's vocabulary: 0 when every structural check passes; 1 with `FAIL ` lines at column 0; 4 with an `UNRUN ` line when `pdftotext` or the cached extraction is unavailable (never 0); 2 on a usage error.
- **Pin.** A9's `case_fetch_sources_match_their_pin` covers every file under `tools/case-fetch/`, so this item adds the `<sha256>  <path>` lines of `test_exhibit_b.py` and the three fixtures to `crates/fa-oracles/data/case_fetch.sha256` and updates `case_fetch.py`'s line in the same change (otherwise fa-oracles' verdict could reuse a stale green run).
- **Rust-side fixtures.** The tool's output on each of the three fixtures is committed as `crates/fa-oracles/tests/fixtures/exhibit_b/rows_9col.toml`, `rows_12col.toml` and `rows_11col.toml` (synthetic test data, embedded through A7a's `src/cases/fixtures.rs` `include_str!` pattern, so fa-oracles never reads `tools/` at test time). M2a–M2c's census, mapping and planted-row tests run on them before OD 2b.

## Tests & logging
**Run.**
- `python3 -B -m unittest discover tools/case-fetch` (A9's gate test runs the same command from fa-oracles, so `cargo test -p fa-oracles` runs these too); this item adds `tools/case-fetch/test_exhibit_b.py`, discovered by the default `test*.py` pattern.
- On the real cache: `python3 -B tools/case-fetch/case_fetch.py exhibit-b blmis` → exit 0; stderr carries one diagnostic line per account.
- `cargo test -p fa-oracles --test case_blmis -- exhibit_b_rows_` (the Rust-side load test).

**Fixtures.** Three small text extractions written for the test, one per layout, under `tools/case-fetch/fixtures/` (`exhibit_b_9col.txt`, `exhibit_b_12col.txt`, `exhibit_b_11col.txt`), each with two synthetic accounts, a header line in its layout's declared headers, a page break, a `*` cell (12-column only), a parenthesized negative, a TRANS row and a footer. Synthetic values only; no real names.

**Unit tests (`tools/case-fetch/test_exhibit_b.py`):**
- `test_glantz_9col_rows`: every row has the §15 fields plus `balance_literal` and a 9-element `cells`; values equal the fixture's;
- `test_square_one_12col_star`: the `*` cell is extracted with `star = true` and its printed amount;
- `test_magnify_11col_rows`: 11 cells per row, the "Six Year Transfers" column mapped;
- `test_parenthesized_amount_negative`: `(4,854,688)` → `-4854688`;
- `test_rerun_byte_identical`: two runs on the unchanged fixture write identical bytes;
- `test_extra_column_fails`: a planted 10th cell on a 9-column row → `FAIL ` line naming complaint, account, page and line; exit non-zero;
- `test_dropped_row_fails`: one dated line removed from the parsed rows (not from the page) → the per-account count check prints `FAIL ` with both counts; exit non-zero;
- `test_unparseable_amount_fails`: `1,23O.00` → `FAIL ` naming the cell;
- `test_unknown_layout_fails`: a complaint id with no `LAYOUTS` entry, and a header line differing by one word, each → `FAIL ` (no inference);
- `test_unmapped_description_fails`: a description outside the token table → `FAIL `, and the description text is not written anywhere;
- `test_balance_disagreement_logged`: a planted $1 balance disagreement → one stderr diagnostic (account, page, line, gap) and exit 0;
- `test_footer_disagreement_logged`: a footer off by $9 → stderr diagnostic, exit 0;
- `test_missing_extraction_reports_unrun`: no cached extraction for a complaint → `UNRUN ` line, exit 4, never 0;
- `test_rust_fixture_matches_tool_output`: the tool's output on each fixture equals its committed `crates/fa-oracles/tests/fixtures/exhibit_b/rows_<n>col.toml` byte for byte (so the two sides cannot drift);
- `test_rows_block_hash`: `rows_sha256` of each fixture complaint equals the literal hex digest written in the test, and one changed amount changes it;
- `test_verify_rows_fact_receipt`: `verify` on a fixture rows fact writes its receipt with `quote_sha256` equal to the block's `rows_sha256` and all four binding booleans true, and a row whose amount is not at its (page, line) in the extraction prints `FAIL ` and exits 1.
- Rust side, `crates/fa-oracles/tests/case_blmis.rs`: `exhibit_b_rows_fixture_loads` loads the three `rows_<n>col.toml` fixtures under `fa_registry::toml::parse` and re-serializes to identical canonical bytes; `exhibit_b_rows_block_hash_matches` recomputes `rows_sha256` over the same block and asserts the same literal hex digest as `test_rows_block_hash` (one value, asserted in both languages).

**On the real cached complaints** the tool logs per-account row counts and the running-sum diagnostics; M2a–M2c compare them with §9.2's figures (for example 1ZR010: closing gap $9, footer 5,979,184 against a row sum of 5,979,193; 1FR048: 1,010/1,050 rows off, max $3).

**Logging.** stdout is data and stderr is diagnostics; every diagnostic names complaint, account, page and line, and every structural failure is a `FAIL ` line at column 0.

## Notes
- **Why each edge exists:**
  - **A9:** the tool, its normalization and its literal rule.
  - **A1a:** the fa-oracles loader the rows file must parse under (the `fa_registry::toml` subset: one-line arrays, basic strings only).
- **Owner decision.** OD 2b gates committing the rows file, not the tool (the bead links the owner-decision bead as `related`). Until it lands, the rows-dependent expectations are unclaimed and print UNRUN (M0's pending-rows rule); the committed fixture is synthetic, so it is not a whole numeric exhibit.
- **Hash definition.** Round 2 wrote the rows fact's hash as "the rows as `fa_registry::toml` canonically re-serializes them"; Python's stdlib has no TOML writer and would need a port of `Document::canonical_bytes` (`crates/fa-registry/src/toml.rs:56-95`) to compute it in `verify`. The block-bytes definition above needs no port and is pinned by one digest asserted in both languages. M2a–M2c use it. A1a (the fact's `quote_sha256`) and A3a (K-BIND's recomputation) still say "the complaint's canonical `[[row]]` bytes"; requires both to read that as this block's exact bytes.
- **Checked at 8196f5e40.** The TOML subset limits (`crates/fa-registry/src/toml.rs:1-9`: one-line arrays, basic strings, no floats or dates); A9's exit vocabulary 0 / 1 / 4 / 2 and its pin test, as its bead states.
- **Fields beyond §15's list.** `balance_literal` and `cells` are added because M-T-01 compares every printed balance and the column-count check needs the printed cells; both are printed values, not inferences.
- **Downstream.** M2a–M2c consume the file.

## Why
Plan: `docs/FORENSIC_CASE_STUDIES_PLAN.md` §15 A3a, §3.6, §2.1, §4.1, §4.4, §4.5, §4.6, Appendix Z.3.

These checks are the program's first defence against its own research record. That record contained:
- quotes that occur in no stored source (EV-040: 0 hits in 22 stored texts);
- account ids that are not in the texts;
- a procedure name that does not exist;
- a misattributed scope (SEC ¶29 applied to the 1Q02 entries) (§4.5, Appendix Z.3).

The plan answers that risk with "sentence-aligned value and scope binding (K-BIND, K-SCOPE); receipts plus independent re-verify. The research's own errors are planted as test fixtures." (§17). Each check is a named, unit-tested refusal of the harness with a planted-violation fixture (§3.6). Every claim type whose basis contains a sourced fact rests on them (D/P/T/C/R/N, §0.3). They run offline. Tests never read the fetch cache (§4.3), and source fidelity is established by the receipt, "reviewable evidence bound to a SHA-256" (§4.6).

## What to build
**Files and public API** (introduced here):
- `crates/fa-oracles/src/cases/check/mod.rs`: `pub struct CheckReport { check: String, verdict: Verdict, examined: usize, inputs: usize, pending: usize, failures: Vec<CheckFailure> }`, `pub enum Verdict { Pass, Fail, Unrun }` (a report with `examined == 0` is `Unrun`, never `Pass`, §3.6), `pub struct CheckFailure { file, line, row, reason }`, and `pub fn run_load_checks(case: &Case) -> Vec<CheckReport>`, to which each later check item adds its checks. `inputs` counts the rows of the check's input kind, `examined` those it checked and `pending` those it could not check because their data waits on OD 2b (below); `examined + pending < inputs` is a FAIL (a skipped row). Its `Display` prints `CHECK <K-ID> PASS|FAIL|UNRUN examined=<n>/<inputs>`, plus ` unrun=<k> (OD 2b)` when `pending > 0`, and then one `FAIL <K-ID> <file>:<line> <row>: <reason>` line per failure, at column 0 (the gate vocabulary of `scripts/lib/gate_verdict.sh`). A10a prints the same line with the case id after `CHECK`.
- `pub fn pending_facts(case: &Case) -> BTreeSet<FactId>`: every `rows_file` fact whose file is not committed (A1a's `rows_committed = false`; whole tables wait on OD 2b, §3.2b), and every fact whose `from` chain (over `derived.from` and `interpretation.from`, transitively) reaches one (M2a's six row-derived facts, M2c's drift facts, M6's declared trial balances). A leg whose `amount_rule` names a pending fact is pending too. K-BIND and K-DERIVE count those rows as pending inputs, never examined and never PASS; M0's rule makes their expectations UNRUN `reason=rows file not committed (OD 2b)` (A7a).
- **Case-scoped checks.** A check that only one case needs (X5's `x_t_03_basis_covers_every_tagged_member` over the Microsoft group facts) is a function in `crates/fa-oracles/src/cases/check/case/<case dir>.rs`, registered in `run_load_checks` through `pub const CASE_CHECKS: &[CaseCheck { case: &'static str, name: &'static str, run: fn(&Case) -> CheckReport }]`. Its id is `case:<case dir>:<name>`; it runs only on that case (and on a `run_planted` copy loaded under that case's id), prints the same `CHECK`/`FAIL` lines, and reports UNRUN when the case has no rows of its input kind. A check that lives only in a test file (`tests/case_xbrl.rs`) never runs in the runner's step 1 (A7a), so it cannot fail a run; the case items' load-time recomputations that must fail a run (X5) register here.
- `crates/fa-oracles/src/cases/check/{prov,bind,scope,anchor,derive}.rs`: one function per check, each returning a `CheckReport`.
- `crates/fa-oracles/src/cases/normalize.rs`: `pub fn normalize(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<String, NormalizeError>` and `pub fn quote_sha256(quote: &str) -> String` (fa-oracles' SHA-256 from A1a over the normalized bytes).
- `crates/fa-oracles/src/cases/sentence.rs`: `pub fn aligned_spans(text: &str) -> Vec<Range<usize>>` (every run of whole sentences), `pub fn is_aligned(text, span) -> bool`, `pub fn occurrences(text, quote) -> usize`, and `pub fn find_by_hash(text, quote_sha256) -> Result<Range<usize>, FindError>` (zero or several matching spans is an error), the same search A9's `verify` runs before OD 2a.
- `crates/fa-oracles/data/binding_fixtures.toml` (embedded; shared with A9's Python tests): `[[normalize]]` rows (`id`, `input_hex`, `normalized_hex`, `quote_sha256`, in hex because the TOML subset has no `\u` escapes), `[[sentence]]` rows (`id`, `text`, `quote`, `aligned`, `count`) and `[[scope]]` rows (`id`, `quote`, `about_scope`, `scope_ok`). A1a creates the file with its `[[values]]` rows (the canonical values bytes behind `values_sha256`); A3a and A9 each add their suites over the same rows, so the Rust and Python implementations are tested on identical bytes.

**The checks (§3.6).**
- **K-PROV.** Every fact has exactly one provenance kind with its required fields (§2.1 table) and values from the closed sets (`provenance`, `granularity`, `anchor_kind`, `role`). An `interpretation` needs `reader` and `second_reader`, and they differ (Si rests on two readers, §2.6 rule 1). A row that mixes two kinds is already refused at load (A1a); K-PROV counts and reports the rows it examined.
- **K-BIND.**
  - **Values.** Every value of a sourced fact occurs literally in the quote after its registered literal rule (A1a `[[value]]` rows, A2 rules): "$742,725,000" → 742725000.00 USD; "$560 million" → the literal 560 with scale 10^6 (§2.1). A value that is not literally in the quote cannot be `sourced`.
  - **Prose anchors** (`page`, `line`, `paragraph`): the quote is one or more whole sentences, unique at the anchor.
  - **Value-bound anchors** (`table_cell`, `xbrl`): there is no sentence; the fact binds by exact value. Its quote is the cell's text or the fact's value, and the value must equal it exactly after the literal rule (§4.1).
  - **Hash.** `quote_sha256` equals `quote_sha256(quote)` when the quote is present. When it is absent (every fact until OD 2a), the receipt records the binding check: K-BIND requires the receipt's `quote_sha256` to equal the fact's and its `values_in_quote`, `aligned` and `quote_found_once` to be true (§4.4 after polish round 2; round 2's `sentence_aligned` is now `aligned`).
  - **Alignment kind.** The receipt's `alignment` must be `sentence` for a prose anchor and `value` for a `table_cell`, `xbrl` or `exhibit` anchor (§4.4; an `exhibit` fact binds by its rows-block hash, below, and each row by value). For `value`, `aligned` means whole-unit binding: the quote is the whole cell or fact value, never a fragment of it.
  - **Whole-exhibit facts** (`rows_file`, A1a): K-BIND recomputes `quote_sha256` as `rows_sha256(complaint)`, the SHA-256 of the exact bytes of that complaint's contiguous `[[row]]` block in the committed rows file, from its first `[[row]]` header line through the newline that ends its last field line (M2t's definition, which Python computes without a TOML writer), never a re-serialization of the parsed rows. While the file is not committed (OD 2b), K-BIND still checks the fact's receipt (its `quote_sha256` equals the fact's and its four booleans are true; a mismatch FAILs) but counts the fact as a pending input, because the block hash has not been recomputed: `unrun=<k> (OD 2b)`, never PASS for it.
  - **Values and scope edited after `verify`.** K-BIND recomputes `values_sha256` from the fact's current `[[value]]` rows and `about_scope` with A1a's `values_canonical` and requires it to equal the receipt's. So a value or scope edited after `verify` fails offline, with no cache (§4.4: "so K-BIND detects offline a value edited after `verify`"); W2's authoring-time negatives become offline locks this way.
- **K-SCOPE.**
  - A quote's own period, entry or account words must not conflict with `about_scope`. The forms are `entry:F12`, `period:2001Q1`, `scheme:capitalization-2001Q1-2002Q1`, `account:1ZR010`.
  - The word check recognizes period phrases with `period_literal`'s patterns, dates with `date_literal`'s, `date_range_literal`'s and `month_literal`'s, and the case's account ids. A named period must equal a `period:` scope, equal an `entry:` scope's sourced reporting period, or lie inside a `scheme:` scope's range; a named account must equal an `account:` scope.
  - **Scheme ranges.** A scheme scope is `scheme:<name>[-<range>]`, and the range is read from the slug's tail, in this order:
    - `<P1>-<P2>`, two quarter labels `YYYYQn` (§2.1's `capitalization-2001Q1-2002Q1`; E2a's `reserve-release-2000Q2-2000Q3`; E2b's `raptor-restructuring-2001Q1-2001Q2`): P1's first day through P2's last;
    - `<M1>-<M2>`, two months `YYYY-MM`; `<YYYY-MM-DD>`, one day (C0's `linkedin-ppa-2016-12-08`); `<YYYY-MM>`, one month (E2b's `notes-for-stock-2001-03`); `<P>`, one quarter label.

    A named quarter lies inside when every one of its days does, and a named date, day range or month when all its days do. A quarter label's days are its calendar quarter, as A2's `period_literal` reads "the second quarter of 2001"; a filer whose fiscal quarters are not calendar quarters writes its scheme bounds as months or days. A tail whose last hyphen-separated component begins with four digits but parses under none of the forms (`2001Q5`, `2001-13`), or a range whose end precedes its start, fails K-SCOPE. A slug with no range (E2c's `spe-ljm1`, E2b's `notes-for-stock`) bounds no period: the quote's named periods and dates are not checked against it (its entry and account words still are), and K-SCOPE's report lists each such fact as `range=none`, so a reader sees which bindings were not range-checked.
  - A scheme-level quote supports an entry-level fact only through an `interpretation` row (§2.1).
  - When the quote is absent, the receipt's `scope_ok` carries the same check, run by A9 with the same word rules.
- **K-ANCHOR.** The anchor kind is one the source's `anchor_scheme` names (`page`, `line`, `paragraph`, `table_cell`, `xbrl`, §4.1, and A1a's `exhibit{label, page range}` for a whole table of rows) and suits the source's `format`; the anchor string parses under A1a's grammar; and the anchor is in range for the recorded original, which the receipt witnesses: for prose kinds the receipt's found range lies inside the fact's declared anchor (same page or paragraph, found lines ⊆ declared lines); for value-bound kinds the receipt's anchor equals the fact's. `exhibit` (`<label>|p<first>-<last>`, M2a's Glantz Exhibits B-1 … B-6) is accepted only on a fact that carries `rows_file` (A1a refuses it elsewhere at load; K-ANCHOR re-checks it on every case), its receipt's `alignment` is `value`, and its receipt anchor must equal the fact's page range.
- **K-DERIVE.** Every derived fact recomputes exactly under its rule with A2's engine (`cases::derive::evaluate`), and so does every leg `amount_rule` of the form `value:` or `derived:` (A1b, and any form A1b adds that names a fact), against the leg's `amount_literal`. A pending fact or leg (`pending_facts`) is counted, not recomputed.

**Normalization (§4.4).** Identical in Python (A9) and Rust, and tested in both on `binding_fixtures.toml`:
- decode UTF-8 (invalid UTF-8 is an error, never replaced);
- replace every run of U+0009, U+000A, U+000B, U+000C, U+000D, U+0020 and U+00A0 with one U+0020;
- trim;
- no case folding, no Unicode normalization, and typographic quotes are kept.

Neither Python's `str.split()` nor Rust's `char::is_whitespace` is used, because they disagree on U+001C–U+001F.

**Sentence alignment (§4.4).**
- A quote starts at the extraction's start, or after `[.?!]` plus an optional closing `"` or `)` plus whitespace.
- It ends with `[.?!]` plus an optional closing `"` or `)`, followed by whitespace or the end.
- A boundary preceded by a registered abbreviation (`p`, `pp`, `Inc`, `Co`, `No`, `U.S`, `Mr`, `Ms`, `Dr`) is not a boundary.
- **Headings.** A heading-only line (SRC-ENR-002's "UNAUDITED" legend, E1; A1a anchors it `page` or `line` on the heading's own line(s)) has no terminator, so the rules above would never align it. A quote is also aligned when it is the whole normalized text of exactly the lines its anchor names and those lines contain no `[.?!]`: the heading is then one whole sentence. Any part of such a line is a fragment, and a quote spanning a heading and the sentence after it must satisfy the ordinary rules. The check needs the anchor's line range, so it runs where the extraction's lines exist (A9's `verify`, before normalization joins them) and is recorded in the receipt's `aligned`; the Rust side checks a present quote against the same `[[sentence]]` fixture rows.

**The Rust side at gate time.** For a fact whose quote is present it checks value occurrence, the hash and the sentence shape itself; exactly-once at the anchor needs the extraction, so it comes from the receipt's `quote_found_once`, because tests never read the cache (§4.3). `original_hash_compared` (copied by A9 from the source row) is carried through to the no-claim, never turned into a pass condition.

**SHA-256.** The std-only module and its FIPS 180-4 vectors land in A1a (§15 A1a). A3a binds it to the normalization (`quote_sha256`) and keeps §15's "FIPS 180-4 vectors fail if altered" negative by calling A1a's `check_vectors` with an altered table from the K-BIND suite.

## Tests & logging
Unit tests in the files above, run by `cargo test -p fa-oracles`; negative names follow `scripts/gate_evidence_check.sh:151-155`.
- **Positive.** `pilot_facts_pass_k_prov_bind_scope_anchor_derive`: on `crates/fa-oracles/tests/fixtures/pilot/` (with its fixture receipts), every check reports PASS with `examined > 0`, and the report lists the examined rows. `normalization_matches_the_shared_fixtures` and `sentence_alignment_matches_the_shared_fixtures` pass on every `binding_fixtures.toml` row.
- **Negatives (§15),** each planted in a test copy of the pilot and each asserting the `FAIL <K-ID> <file>:<line> <row>: <reason>` line:
  - `sec_p29_quote_bound_to_f12_fails_k_scope`: a sourced fact with `about_scope = "entry:F12"` whose quote contains the ¶29 phrase "without any supporting documentation or proper business rationale" and the scope words "for the first quarter of 2001" (SEC v. Vinson & Normand, a government work, §1.3) → `FAIL K-SCOPE … names period 2001Q1, entry F12 is 2002Q1`;
  - `entered_and_approved_fragment_fails_k_bind_alignment`: the fragment "entered and approved" inside a synthetic carrier sentence with a role label in place of the name ("The director rarely entered and approved entries himself.") → `FAIL K-BIND … not sentence-aligned`. No name-bearing quote text is committed (OD 3);
  - `quote_occurring_twice_fails_k_bind`: a fixture extraction where the quote's sentence appears twice → `occurrences = 2`, `FAIL K-BIND … found 2 times`;
  - `altered_fips_vector_fails`: `check_vectors` on a table with one altered digit returns `Err` naming the vector.
- **Derived negatives** (§3.6, §4.1, §4.4):
  - `receipt_range_outside_the_fact_anchor_fails_k_anchor` (fact `p111:l12-14`, receipt `p112:l3-4`);
  - `anchor_kind_not_in_the_source_scheme_fails_k_anchor` (`xbrl` on a `pdf` source whose scheme is `["page"]`);
