A research decision record that answers:
- which mechanism, if any, should exist: a kernel law (a refusal at commit) or an intent-bound Canon rule family (which needs intent-bound rule families, absent at 8196f5e40, `registries/verbs.toml:664`);
- what it would read: an asset debit paired with an equity credit for own-stock issuance against a note (the documented form Dr notes receivable / Cr shareholders' equity), with the account types and roles involved; the equity/contra-equity role mapping is today an interpretive `role_map` rule (§7.2 E-T-03);
- its claim class (a law refuses; it cites the invariant it exercises and never inherits `invariant` class for a case claim, §0.3);
- the follow-on product bead if one is adopted. That bead, when it lands, flips E-N-01: the N fails as designed, is retired with a `Retired-tests:` trailer (§16), and the case gains a row whose twin is law-level.

It cites no accounting standard number unless one is sourced. It never proposes a test-only product path (§3.4) as the mechanism.

**Where the record lives.** As one comment on this bead (`br comments add <id> …`), the tracker's record, as the owner-decision bead records decisions. Its fixed headings:
- **Question**;
- **Entry form**: the two Powers-documented notes-for-stock entries ($172M, April 2000; $828M, March 2001; pp.125–126), encoded as E3's `native` book encodes them;
- **Options**, one per mechanism, each with what it reads and the code that would host it:
  - a kernel law: a step of `prepare_post_in`, `crates/fa-journal/src/post.rs:1575`;
  - an intent-bound Canon family: `crates/fa-canon`, after intent-bound families exist;
- **Claim class**;
- **E-T-03 check**: E-T-03's reversing Δ stays admissible under the proposed law;
- **Recommendation**;
- **Follow-on bead id or "none"**.

The bead's close reason cites the comment. No `docs/` file is created: the repository has no research-record convention under `docs/`.

A Lantern detector is not among §13's H7 options. A record that considers one states that a detector refuses nothing, so E-N-01 (a `no_refusal` N, E3: it passes when the intent is admitted) would still hold, and a detector would add a D row instead of flipping E-N-01.

**If a follow-on product bead is filed,** its acceptance must name:
- an additive `registries/refusal_catalog.toml` row (a new `FA-REF-*` code with its exit class);
- the invariant the law exercises, with a live checker in `registries/invariants.toml` (AGENTS.md: no subsystem ships against an unenforced invariant);
- the account role or type rows the law reads (`registries/account_roles.toml` / `account_types.toml`);
- a `fa-reference` model of the law, so A4r's differential reports `agree` rather than `not_modelled` (`crates/fa-reference/src/lib.rs:1-20` mirrors the kernel's law order);
- a planted negative: the same entry with the mechanism's input flipped is admitted;
- the flip of E-N-01 stated above.

Every registry edit invalidates every crate verdict (`scripts/crate_verdicts.py:13-15`).

## Tests & logging

- Research item: it closes on the decision record (a bead comment), not on code. Any follow-on product change lands with its own tests (the law, a planted negative, its registry rows), not here.
- Any source quoted in the record is retrieved through `tools/case-fetch/case_fetch.py` (A9) or with the project User-Agent `franken_accounting-agent (+https://github.com/Dicklesworthstone/franken_accounting)` (AGENTS.md), and its SHA-256 is recorded.
- Until a mechanism lands, E-N-01's runner line stays `PASS|FAIL|UNRUN E-N-01 N native <target>/<cell> <projection-with-statuses> [reason=…]` (A7a's line, one per target and cell) with the harness-observability twin's status (E3).

## Notes

- **No dependencies** (§15: H7 ← none). The edge E3 ← H7 was removed from the plan: E3 runs E-N-01 with the harness-observability twin (§7.2; §15 E3 "harness-observability twin"). A `related` link to E3 records that this bead's landing flips E-N-01.
- **Plan vs code, settled.** The plan's earlier "planted rule in a test-only Canon policy" twin is gone. Canon rules map a recorded REA event to candidate legs (`crates/fa-canon/src/lib.rs:1-3`), so a Canon answer needs an intent-bound rule family first; the record must say so if it recommends Canon.
- E-T-03 uses the same entries; a future pairing law would read the same legs, so the record checks that E-T-03's Restatement Δ (Dr equity / Cr notes receivable, the reversal of the two entries) stays admissible under it.
- A13's coverage accounting records the gap as `law_absent`, naming this bead.
- **fa-c34 (fa-canon first slice, in progress)** owns the machinery a Canon answer would use: its item 4 is "Rule families bound to intents: Accrue/Defer, Allocation, Revaluation, Elimination", and its 2026-10-23 comment lists "rule families bound to intents" among the parts not yet built. A contra-equity pairing family is not among them. So a Canon recommendation from this record is a follow-on family on fa-c34's item-4 machinery, filed against fa-c34, never a second rule engine; a kernel-law recommendation does not touch fa-c34. No bead plans the contra-equity pairing law itself.

## Why

Product finding H13 (docs/FORENSIC_CASE_STUDIES_PLAN.md §13): PolicyChangeRetrospective posting has no test anywhere (`crates/fa-journal/src/case.rs:1661-1675`). PolicyChangeRetrospective cases post through the same RESTATEMENT class, but no test posts one yet (§3.3 step 5). The Microsoft FY2018 case (§10.2) recasts revenue, net income and equity under ASC 606 / ASC 842 as a PolicyChangeRetrospective case (X-T-03, X-R-02), which "would be its first exercise". §17 lists it with A0 as a missing product surface that stays UNRUN until it lands. H13 blocks X5.

## What to build

Two tests, no product code unless a test exposes a defect (see Notes).

**1. Library-level test** in `crates/fa/tests/restatement_case.rs`, introducing `a_policy_change_retrospective_case_restates_a_locked_periods_posting_under_independent_approvals`. It is modelled on `a_restatement_case_restates_a_locked_periods_posting_under_independent_approvals` (`:1184`) and reuses the file's `book(seed)` fixture (`:62`), `day` (`:58`) and `assert_posted_diff_is_the_rehearsed_one` (`:27`). It runs the case through open (kind `PolicyChangeRetrospective`), determine, rehearse (the retained scenario diff certificate), approve (two Human approvals, no planner approves, approvers with no shared session lineage), post, disclose and close.
- This is the half that exercises session lineage over issued capabilities. The CLI cannot, because an issued token cannot approve on the CLI (`FA-REF-011`; the comment at `crates/fa-cli/tests/cli.rs:4516-4519`).

**2. CLI test** in `crates/fa-cli/tests/cli.rs`, introducing `a_policy_change_retrospective_case_restates_a_closed_period_from_the_cli`. It is modelled on `a_restatement_case_restates_a_closed_period_from_the_cli` (`:4512-4733`) and drives the real binary with the file's `fa()` helper (`:78-96`, every `--json` line schema-checked):
1. `fa case open --kind PolicyChangeRetrospective --periods <code> --determined-on <d> --authority <oid>` (usage at `registries/verbs.toml:251`);
2. `fa scenario fork` / `fa scenario run` with a Restatement intent whose `restates` names the posting, then `fa scenario diff … --certificate-out <file>`;
3. `fa case rehearse <case> --certificate <file>`;
4. `fa case approve <case> --dry-run`, then two `fa approve --proposal <digest> --as <non-planner>`, then `fa case approve <case> --approval <v1> --approval <v2>`;
5. `fa case post <case>` by a non-approver;
6. `fa case disclose <case> --document <oid>` (a Disclosure document reviewed by a principal other than its uploader);
7. `fa case close <case>`.

**Expected behaviour (each Δ).** The Δ is a Restatement-class entry that `restates` a named posting. It is admitted only in Closed or Locked periods as `with:restate,2` (`registries/admissibility.toml:380-401`), with a registered supersession reason. After the post, `fa case diff <case> --statements --certify` retains the statement-diff certificates, and `fa explain lineage` names the Restates edge.

## Tests & logging

- **Positive (both tests):**
  - the PCR case posts into a Closed (CLI) or Locked (library) period;
  - `fa tb` for the period moves by exactly the Δ;
  - `fa explain lineage <target>` names Restates;
  - `fa case show` reports Closed;
  - in the CLI test, `fa replay C:<oid>` of the statement-diff certificate reproduces its robot line byte for byte (FA-INV-11).
- **Negatives, mirroring the Restatement CLI test,** each asserted on its exact refusal:
  - `pcr_case_with_one_approval_is_refused_fa_ref_012`: one approval → `FA-REF-012` (exit 7), and `fa case show` still reports Rehearsed;
  - `pcr_intent_with_revises_instead_of_restates_is_refused`: a `revises` in place of `restates` on the planned intent → usage error (exit 2);
  - `pcr_restatement_outside_its_case_is_refused_fa_ref_178`: the same intent posted with `fa post` outside the case → `FA-REF-178` (exit 3, CASE_MEMBERSHIP).
- Test names carry `refused`/`FA-REF-NNN` so the gate-evidence ratchet counts them (`scripts/gate_evidence_check.sh:151-155`).
- `cargo test -p fa --test restatement_case` and `-p fa-cli --test cli` output; no `#[ignore]` (§16). The CLI test logs each step's argv and exit, as the precedent test does.

## Notes

- **No dependencies** (§15: H13 ← none). **Blocks X5** (X5 ← H13).
- **Ownership agrees across the plan:** §10.2 ("Item H13 lands a library-level test first, and X5 depends on it"), §13 and §15 all assign the library-level PolicyChangeRetrospective test to H13, which X5 waits on.
- If the tests expose a defect in PCR posting, H13 does not close on a failing or ignored test (§16). The defect is fixed here, with the fix's own regression, or filed as a blocker bead that H13 waits on.
- No registry or surface changes: `case open --kind PolicyChangeRetrospective` is already in the usage (`registries/verbs.toml:251`), so verbs.toml, MCP parity and the robot-schema pin are untouched.
- **Landing gates.** `bash scripts/tier0.sh` before main; `cargo test -p fa --test restatement_case` and `-p fa-cli --test cli`.
- **H12 interaction:** H12 corrects the `case post` usage to say PCR posts Restatement entries; this bead lands the first test that shows it.
- **fa-75j4 (RestatementCase life cycle, in progress, MaroonCat)** owns case posting; the tree read for polish round 4 has its PriorPeriodAdjustment, Revision and Restatement case tests (`crates/fa/tests/restatement_case.rs:593`, `:922`, `:1184`; `crates/fa-cli/tests/cli.rs:4214`, `:4524`; the CLI Restatement test's doc comment, `:4512`, attributes it to fa-651f slice 1) but none for PolicyChangeRetrospective, and no test file in the tree read for polish round 4 names the kind (`rg PolicyChangeRetrospective crates/*/tests` is empty). No bead plans the PCR test, so H13 carries it; the tests are modelled on the existing Restatement tests and announced on fa-75j4's thread, since a defect they expose lands in fa-75j4's code.

## Why

Product finding H17 (docs/FORENSIC_CASE_STUDIES_PLAN.md §13; §6.4 W-D-07/W-D-08): Lantern populations keep a restated target and include its Restatement entries, and findings do not mention the correction lineage. Restates lineage does not remove a target from `live_postings` (`crates/fa-lantern/src/snapshot.rs:505-512`); `live_postings` keeps every class except Reversal, and `jet.round_amounts` does not filter by class (`crates/fa-lantern/src/jet.rs:102`), nor does `jet.post_closing` (`jet.rs:249`). So after WorldCom's native restatement, `expense.capitalization` still cites every corrected entry (W-D-07) and `jet.round_amounts` cites the correction's own entries (W-D-08): "a correction looks like the entry it corrects to these predicates". These are FA's own false positives on a real-shaped book, shown deliberately. The research question: should findings carry supersession lineage, as `shares_supersession_lineage` does for duplicates?

## What to build

A research decision record covering `expense.capitalization`, `jet.round_amounts` and `jet.post_closing`:
- whether findings should carry the supersession lineage of a cited entry (for example naming the Restates edge and the case), whether a procedure should treat a restated target or a Restatement entry differently, or neither;
- the precedents:
  - `Snapshot::shares_supersession_lineage` (`crates/fa-lantern/src/snapshot.rs:525`), used by the duplicates procedures (`crates/fa-lantern/src/duplicates.rs:85`, `:171`, `:421`);
  - the filter `live_postings_through` already applies (`snapshot.rs:509-513`): it drops Reversal-class entries, reversed targets and *replaced* targets, but not *restated* ones. So the asymmetry between Replace and Restates lineage is in one predicate, and the record states whether it is intended;
- the constraint that the corrected entry stays in the journal (append-only, AGENTS.md doctrine 5);
- the follow-on product bead if a change is adopted, including the procedure version bumps it implies.

**Where the record lives.** As one comment on this bead (`br comments add <id> …`), the tracker's record, as the owner-decision bead records decisions. Its fixed headings:
- **Question**;
- **Observation**: W7's W-D-07/W-D-08 runner lines and their projections, cited by expectation id;
- **Options per procedure** (annotate findings; filter restated targets or Restatement entries under a version bump; neither);
- **Effect on W-D-07/W-D-08**;
- **Recommendation**;
- **Follow-on bead id or "none"**.

The bead's close reason cites the comment. No `docs/` file is created: the repository has no research-record convention under `docs/`.

**If a follow-on product bead is filed,** its acceptance must name, per procedure whose output moves:
- the `registries/procedures.toml` `version` bump, with the code's `fn version` to match (`crates/fa-lantern/src/lib.rs:1015-1018` refuses a mismatch);
- the certificate tables: `CERTIFIABLE_PROCEDURES` (`crates/fa/src/procedure_certificate.rs:47`, where `jet.round_amounts` sits at `:48` and `jet.post_closing` at `:50`) and `EXTENDED_CERTIFIABLE_PROCEDURES` (`crates/fa/src/procedure_certificate/extended.rs:16`, where `expense.capitalization` sits at `:49`);
- fresh two-reader tier rows (K-TIER, A4);
- H9's calibration rows;
- a fa-lantern test with a planted negative: a restated target, or a Restatement entry, that the new predicate treats as designed.

Only annotating findings would change the finding payload; the follow-on states whether that is a version change too.

## Tests & logging

- Research item: it closes on the decision record, with W7's W-D-07/W-D-08 runs as the observation. Any follow-on product change lands with its own tests. Its effect on W-D-07/W-D-08 is reported through the runner (`PASS|FAIL|UNRUN W-D-07 D native <target>/<cell> <projection-with-statuses> [reason=…]`, one line per (expectation, target, cell), A7a), and any change to those rows follows the §16 trailers.

## Notes

- **← W7:** the native restatement and its W-D-07/W-D-08 rows are the observation this research reads.
- **If a change is adopted:** procedure versions bump, which invalidates their tier rows (K-TIER, A4) and needs fresh two-reader classifications. W-D-08's cites could fall, which counts as weakening under §16 (fewer cites) and needs a `Weakened-tests:` trailer. W-D-07's rendering could name the lineage from the finding itself rather than from `fa explain lineage`.
- `jet.post_closing` is tier P, so in the case it renders only in Limits, as the note W-NOTE-02 (all components X; an all-X row is not an expectation, PLAN_DELTA after polish round 2; §6.4, §15 W4).
- H9's calibration rows are keyed by procedure version and would follow any bump.
- Every registry edit a follow-on makes invalidates every crate verdict (`scripts/crate_verdicts.py:13-15`).

